        <head>AGONY OF PILES. A WONDERFUL ZA-M-BUK CURE.</head>
        <p>THE AGONY OF PILES. A WONDERFUL ZA-M-BUK CURE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Rich, a young married woman living at 82, Erskine-road, Walthamstovv, London, E., suffered severely from piles for ten years, but in a comparatively short time she was perfectly cured by Zam-Buk, the greatest healer in modern science. " I suffered periodically from piles ever since 17 years of age, palticularly badly after confine- ment," says Mrs Rich. " They were described as being internal and external piles,and the parts were inflamed and very sore. "I tried pile ointment, nutgall ointment and other cheap salves, and pile pills. Three doctors, one after the other, prescribed lotions and oint- ments, but my complaint was utterly beyond the akrof these things. I happened to read a Zam- Buk booklet and suggested to my husband that be shonld get me a box. At that time I could neither sit, lie, nor sleep, and I was terribly bad. " When my husband brought the Zam-Buk I made two applications the same evening by warming the Zam-Buk on a piece of lint and it was so soothing and relieved me so much that I was actually able to sleep. After this I applied the balm frequently, and very soon the pains and swellings lessened and then vanished, and I came to be entirely free from discomfort. After some perseverance, I realised at last that 1- was com- pletely cured. " What strikes me as most pleasing about my cure is the fact that it has been permanent. I effected the cure last April and there is absolutely no sign of the return of the disease. "I attribute my cure solely to Zam-Buk and I can conscientiously recommend it to others. " My husband" added Mrs. Rich, "has also used Zam-Buk for a sprain which he sustained through falling off his bicycle, and he, too, found it very beneficial. A large bruise on his ankle was better the next day, thanks to the rapid heal- ing and soothing powers of Zam-Buk."</p>
        <p>It took the Rev. Robert Stone just one week of duty in Saffron Hedley to make himself the most neterious vicar in the whole of the Lyncester diocese. Hitherto, Saffron Hedley had been wrapped in the obscurity of its own insignificance, though, withal, it prided itself greatly upon its circumspection and ultra-respectability. Ibis, indeed, at least in regard to Church matters, was not to be wondered at, seeing that the Bishop himself had established a country retreat in the neighbourhood, and thus cast the mantle of intellectual and highly respectable ecclesiasticism over the whole locality. Robert Stone had not been trained in such an at mosphere. He had taken Orders in the English Church ten or teelve years ago, but instead of accepting a curacy in England, fell in love with a scheme for the evangelisation of California, and had promptly migrated to help carry it out. The whole of his clerical career, therefore, had been spent in mining camps, pioneering the Gospel by the aid of the first catechism and fisticuffs. And it must be said for his efforts that the promulga- tion of muscular religion had been attended with signal success. He loved his work, and a pressing appeal from his father to come back and accept the living at ' Saffron Hedley, in the gift of an uncle, seals answered promptly and laconically by wire: "Too busy. Can't come," the sending off of which created excitement and intense satisfaction in One • Mile. But only a week later came a black-edged envelope. He opened it with nervous fingers, to find his mother was dead, and to learn that it was her dying wish that he should return home and take up work at Saffron Hedley. Such a wish was, to him, an imperative com- mand. Two days later he started for home, and Stockton Heath Chemist's Application, the rough miners turned out in force to see him mount the box of the next coach to Los Angelos. The annual licensing sessions for the petty sessional They dumped alongside him a testimonial surprise- division of Daresbury were held at the Hall Cafd, bag of gold-dust and bade him "God-speed " in Stockton Beath, on Tuesday. Mr. H. Nield presided , husky, sorrowful voices, and with genuine tears of and the Bench consisted of Messrs. G.Pilling, J. White, regret in their eyes. R. C. Parr, J. Hepherd, L. Greening, H. Roberts, and i He arrived at Saffron Hedley on a Saturday C. Whitley , night, a couple of days before he had been SUPERINTENDENT'S REPORT—A RENEW. L ADJOURNED. expected. It had been arranged for a stranger to Superindent Ennion reported that there were twenty conduct the services of the following day, and the licensed victuallers and three beer sellers in the division, giving an average of 391 of the population to new vicar fidgeted restlessly all through the morn- each licence. No licenced victuallers were proceeded ing service, and in the evening put in no against during the year, a decrease of two proceeded appearance. against and one convicted on the preceding year. 1 As the worshippers at this church passed the One beerseller had been proceeded against , Vicarage after service that night, their eyes beheld and convicted, an increase of one. Twenty-three a strange sight. persons had been proceeded against for drunkenness, i The study blinds were not drawn, and silhouetted twenty-two of whom were convicted, " against a lamp, tilted back in a chair, with his compared with fifteen proceeded against and thirteen feet crossed airily upon the edge of the mantel- convicted on the proceeding year. Of the twenty-three . p twenty were apprehended and the others summoned, piece, sat the new vicar, the Rev. Robert Stone, Seventeen were labourers, one a clerk, one a teamsman, enthusiastically puffing at a huge corn cob and one a skilled workman, one a farmer, one a married utterly absorbed in an interesting book. He didn't woman and one a tramp. Fifteen were residents and like the service, he told the squire who waited on eight non-residents. With one exception