he people Life Wrecked by Indigestion and Biliousness. ' were somewhat pleasurably disappointed. - But the expected upexpected happened in the Chas. Forde's Bile Beans a Real Life-saver. , evening, when the Rev. Robert Stone presented • himself at the reading-desk with a bandage across his forehead and a discolouration above his right cheek bone which bore an unaccountable similarity to a black eye in its initial stages. There was a hush like the grave when the hymn before the sermon having been sung, the vicar fet remained out of the pulpit. "Friends," he said, "there will be no sermon to- night. lam feeling too unwell and mentally unfit to preach. You are wondering why my head is bandaged and my face discoloured. The explana- tion of that is no doubt current in the village by this time. I leave such of you as are interested sufficiently to learn the story for yourselves. I will now pronounce the benediction.' The congregation surged into the churchyard, and a babel of tor pea ensued among the graves. There was much spemlation, much rumour. It remained to one man, an old woodcutter, Danny Bride, to give the correct version of the</p>
        <p>Now been a Stranger to Sickness 7 Years. "Indigestion, biliousness, and liver and stomach disorder reduced me to the feebleness of a child, but Chas. Forde's Bile Beans lifted me up to the strength of a giant." So says Mr. Thomas Watson, 10, Victor-street, Cutsyke, Castleford, Yorks. ; and his cure, com- plete as it now has been for seven whole years, is clear proof of the fact that Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are no ordinary medicine, but a remedy of merit higher than anything else known. Mr. Watson is a fine type of miner; a champion weight-lifter and strong man of local repute, and is widely respected throughout the important ruining county of South Yorks. " When I was thirty-sex en years old," says Mr. Watson, " I began to suffer from foss of appetite, nervous shaking and dizziness, spots and hltirs running across the sight, and fits of faint. ing. These became worse in spite of all my doctors could do, and at last I was so helpless that I had to be assisted from bed to a chair, where I sat for hours at a time, powerless to move. My doctor told me I would nerer be able to trot* again. Be sent me to the infirmary, where again they failed to do me good. I was so depressed I could not help wishing to die. Then I commenced to take Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. Gradually my strength returned, the distressing symptoms one by one disappeared, and in a short time I was not only able to go back to work, but could lift a 56-pound weight above my head with one finger only, and could do again all the old weight-lifting as well as in my strongest days. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans saved my life, and enabled me again to earn my " That was nearly seven years ago, and I have serer bad a day's illness since. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans renewed my strength and have helped me to keep strong and well ever since." Mr...Tatson made this Sworn Declaration of the statement before a Commissioner for Oaths, in which declaration he verified the whole facts stated above :—" I, Thomas Watson, residing at 10, Victor. street, Cntsyke, Yorkshire, do hereby solemnly and sincerely declare as follows : that from being a man able to lift up two hundred- weights above my head with one arm alone, I was reduced by indigestion and kindred ailments to the weakness of a child unable to walk without assistance. My health has been fully restored and my strength renewed until lam able again to lift heavy weights with ease, solely through taking Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. " And I make this Solemn Declaration, con- scientiously believing the same to be true and in accordance with the Statutory Declarations Act, 1835. (Signed) TitomAs WATSON. " Declared at Casileford, in the County of York, before me (signed), Thomas Wilson, Commissioner for Oaths." Here we have proof that ems. Foltpr.'S Bile Beans are the greitest medicine ever placed within the reach of the public. Because they do more than ordinary physic, they bring new lutpe and new strength to all who try them.</p>
        <p>story. "I tell 'cc I seen en myself, with my own eyes, and 'twas Dicky Tansom what done it. ' There was a groan of dismay among Danny's auditors. You might put one point of a huge compass on Saffron Ifedley and draw a circle with a ten-mile radius and you wouldn't enclose a bigger bully than Dicky Tansom, ex-prize tighter, sot, and county bltsekguard. Everybody knew Dicky, everybody feared him. "Shame ! Shame ! " called out some of the people. "An' im a new-corner, too. Somebody ought to 'a warned him of Dicky." It was, in fact, one of the regular customs of the town to warn new-corners of Dicky Tansom. Those who heeded the warning generally congratu- lated themselves afterwards, when they knew the Tr:M. tiron their circumspection in avoiding him. Those who fell foul of him once rarely repeated the ex' eriment. "Don't 'cc cry shame too soon," spoke up Danny. "Wait till 'ee've 'earcl the yarn. Well, Dicky 'ad just lot Vick fro' Market Kingley, primed up as usual after his Sunday three-mile ' travellin, and soment or other didn't please en, so he started the regular game on the old 'ooman and kicks she out of doors. "' Theer,' he says, punchin' 'ed and 'elpin' 'Sr over the doorstep, that'll teach 'ee to mind 'once a bit Letter.' "lie was followin' her out, ponchin' her all the while, uhen, who should come along but the new parson. "‘ My man my loan "" he says, you're for- gettin' yourself. Don't strike a woman,' and 'e jumps in between Dick and his wife. And why the dets-e not, if I wants to? ' said Dicky. She's my own wife and I'll punch her if I like. Stand out of the way I ' "And, shovin' the parson aside, he brought his list down hard upon his old woman's head and knocked her senselt SR on the stones in the yard. "That was more than the parson could stand, it was, and 'e jumps in front of Dicky : You shan't do that again while I'm 'ere,' he attys. It's part of my business to look after poor ut men like this. l've come here to teach the Gospel, and I reckrn this comes under the heading. I'm going to thrash some of this coward brute out uf you for a start,' "You should 1...1 seen the colour change in Dicky's face. It. een many a yrar sin' lie were A HUGE PIKE AT COMBERMERE. spoke to like that, iii warrant. He tried to laugh On Thursday afternoon last week Mr. Clem. Gold- the thing off tho', and said : bourne, of Audlem, captnred a monster pike in Comber- I "'Now then, you sky-pilot, you just get out of mere. He was spinning in the lower part of the lake my backyard. I don't fight rassons.' when the fish struck his bait,and feeling the hook made off at a tremendous pace. The fisherman cleverly said the passon, • I don't suppose you ; women are more in your line,' and with that handled him, and after half-an-hour's fight brought him he give Dicky the benefit of a blow full on the to the boat's side. He measures three feet three inches hest. Dicky, he staggered back a foot in length, and twenty inches in girth. This is probablyor hoc:, tchorot3 kl startled. It's_about.the first f ono of the largest pikes caught in Comhermere, which Mr. Gould- eweight o mese slaty rlt the V' r a good manly list ingr he'. is reptored to contain many monster fish. bout ne is haying tie pike set up for exhibition purposes. rea,T,l'll all II ett second Or two just to get over his • .... W.l?et:</p>
