rth be possible to him of having money, and the mooted idea that, worship. , having recently returned from the goldfields he The squire made bold to offer a word of might have brought a bag of gold-dust with him, seasonable advice to the new vicar, was treated with ridiculous scorn. Men always "Sir," said he, "such • strange conduct as yours changed their dust before starting for England. will alienate the whole parish. That—ah—ah— Besides, the vicar had been away in the North of unfortunate contretemps with that wretched man England for more than a fortnight. Tansom, for instance, is the talk of the place." So the matter remained a mystery for a month, 1 "But he was thrashing a woman—his wife, and then, ixactly four weeks later, another What would you have done had you been in my mysterious supply of registered envelopes came place ?" , through the post to Saffron Hedley, and again old The squire hesitated. He was nothing of a Samuels found his mail-bag weighted by the linen coward, and he had a high regard for women. But envelopes laden with gold-dust. The month after his courage was politic. He held it in admirable it was the same, and on through the winter while restraint for fitting occasions. i the distress lasted. Local and even London papers "I think," he said, at length, "there are many heard of the mysterious distributions of gold-dust little things.to..svhich it is wise to close our eyes ' in Saffron Heeljey,,aud.started all aorts.nf,theories</p>
        <p>- returnen the vicar; "Dot not when a woman is being brutally hounded to death by a man. Perhaps mine are regarded as strange ideas in this country. In my last parish I never saw but one woman, and she was a clipping off the worst fringe of New York society who had fled the devil in her own city to try and do better with some relatives at 'Frisco. Some strange luck caused her to drop on One Mile Camp, my parish. She hadn't been in the place, an hoar before there were twenty huts vacated for her. She stayed with us— this off-scouring of New York—for close upon three weeks. Never but once, in a camp of the roughest men in America, was she offered an insult, and the man who offered it died thirty minutes later with a bullet in his head. It's in such a school I've had my clerical experience. You must pardon me if I fail to shake off some of my old habits."</p>
        <p>ann rivuutay was any tile wiser in the end. But in his snug study at the Vicarage the Rev. Robert Stone laughed loudly and long, and weighed out the dust which "the boys " had given him, and gave the envelopes over to the pretty school- mistress, who again passed them on to a friend to address and post, and the trio gloated over the fun and gloried in the game of doinz good by stealth. It struck the vicar, too, that "the boys" in One Mile would glory with him in such an hilar;-ms piece of deception, and ha sent one of the papers containing an account, and a very wide solution of the mystery over to Briar Joe, judge and chid _deacon of One Mile City. But "the boys " did not laugh so much over th.? joke. Instead, by the next mail came two :mall botiespacked with nuggets and dust. The wiper contained the name of Squire Hawley, as chairman ot the Distress Fund Committee, and to him th‘ boxes were addressed. Inside was a letter: "Dear Squire,—One box to be distributed among the poor of Saffron liedley, the other to be banked for the Rev. Robert Stone. Yours faithfully, The Church and Council a One Mile City. "Signed,—BaLla jora." s,</p>
        <p>A thrill re.n :hro.oglt the old squire's frame. He was a creature of custom, but he loved grit. lie took Stone's hand.</p>
        <p>"You're right, vicar. Go on with the conversion of this parish in your own way. I shall raise no further objection."</p>
        <p>Atter the excitement emanating from this unique episode had abated the vicar proceeded, without very glaringly outraging the proprieties for upwards of a month. Then he suddenly surprised, not only the parish, but himself. He fcll in love—un- reservedly, head over heels in love—and the object of his affections WIN an orphan girl, whose face and figure were sufficient excuse for the vicar's sudden delirium, but whose position in Saffron Healey was summed up by Mrs. Ashton-Selby's cynical description of her as "that elementary school-teacher, the Lawson girl."</p>
        <p>'The squire, who had suspected the vicar, rejoiczi in this confirmation of his suspicions, and as the committee met that afternoon he announced his gifts and his solution of the mystery to tile members. It took less titan an hour after that for the news to get round the village, and that night the vicar was startled to find his house besieged by a huge crowd of men, women, and children, who persisted in giving ringing cheers, three times three, and three times three again for the "gold-dust vicar," as they styled hint. ft er this he would have been a bold man who</p>
        <p>It was true Mildred Tawson's parentage was not high, but the vicar failed te see how she could be blamed for that; and as she hadn't a relative alive, he saw no future cause of offence. The Rev.</p>
        <p>• had dared to say one word in the village against the vicar. He was the people's idol. He could draw them, harangue them, caution them, chide them, mould them at sweet Aci ii. .</p>
        <p>- Robert Stone looked more kindly upon sweetness of Bill with the return of spring came the aristo- disposition and a beautiful character than upon cratic section of his flock, and the Ashton-Selby wealth or pedigree. No man in One Mile received set found itself absolutely unable, in view'of the homage because he struck luckier veins than vicar's forthcoming matrimonial alliance, to do another, and the vicar could not bring hinuelf to anything but snub and neglect their spiritual estimate the world on other than the One Mie adviser. basis. '.l here was, however, a way of vanquishing even It satisfied him to know that he was desperately ' the Ashton-Selbys, and it came unexpectedly. in love with Millie, and when, taking his first I The district of which Seffron lledley was the opportunity, he discovered that his affection was ' centre had long been in need of an efficient water returned, he voted himself the happiest man in or supply. At the back of the village rose a high out of Great Britain. down, and it had been thought by surveying It never struck his unsophisticated mind to stop geologists that in this down could be discovered the ,and consider what Saffron Ifedley might think of spring of water so greatly needed. the match.. But Flaffrpn Hadley had its.thouchts I This report of the geologists was acted upon, and Mr...Ashton-SelbY. whnas renutation. in. uch</p>
        <p>matters was great, was commissicnea to experi- ment. He sent an army of men to the spot, artesian wells were sunk, and after months of labour the joyful report went forth that water had been found. An enormous crowd assembled upon the top of the down upon a chosen day to give the newly- discovered water a public reception. The Bishop was present to bless the gift; a brass band headed a procession from Saffron ledley, and the whole country side was en fête. Everything passed off with eclat until the time came for turning on the water. To do this it was first necessary that a man should descend a ladder into the surface pit, which had been dug before the sinking of the well shaft. Ashton-Selby gave the word to one of his men, who instantly clambered down the ladder to some scaffolding eight or nine feet below the surface, whence he put foot upon a second ladder which took him another twenty feet to the bottom. Here was a turncock which only had to be manipulated, and the water would burst gloriously forth before the gaze of the waiting multitude. A hush fell upon the people, and they waited breathlessly for the stream to appear. Fifteen seconds, thirty, passed, yet no water made its appearance. "Whatever can he be muddling at ? " snapped Ashton-Selby to his foreman. "It's simple enough. What a slow, lazy fellow. You go down yourself, Jones."</p>
        <p>Jones obeyed, and vanished into the darkness, yet the people saw no more signs of water than they could have expected in the Sahara. What could be the matter? Had the much vaunted spring of water proved too weak to force its way up the pipe to the surface ? "I thought I was right," said one of the High- way Commissioners who had opposed the boring scheme. "They will never find water on this down if they bore through to Australia for it. I always said so, and now here's the proof. They must have tapped some old stone drain, and anyone with half a grain of sense would have kno•vn that that would not be enough to satisfy half-a-dozen parishes." Mr. Ashton-Selby overheard these remarks, as it was intended that he should. He was furious, though he made no answer to the man who had spoken. "That cursed fool, Jones, I'll discharge the man," he said. "The water worked all right yesterday when I came up here to inspect it. Is the man never going to turn it on? Hang it all, I'll go down myself." He suited the action to the word, swung himself over the edge of the pit, and quickly descended to the first platform, and thence into the black chamber beneath. _</p>
        <p>Beware of Substitutes; ask for "OWBRIDGB "when buying Lung Tonic and TAKE NO OTHER.</p>
        <p>.r-'nu a 'femme mapsea, nut still no water came, and an uncanny feeling, vague and indefinite as yet, took possession of everybody. "Selby, Selby," shouted the squire with all the force of his lungs. "Selby. What's the matter? Why don't you answer?" The voice echoed down the- well, but there was no reply. Then like a flash the horror of what had occurred dawned upon the. workmen. "It's the gas, the choke," they cried in horror. "Where's a light." . . . .</p>
        <p>- A little tin lamp, used for lowering into such boles was brought, fastened to a rope worked over a windlass, and slowly lowered. It burnt in safety until after it had passed through the gap in the first platform. Then, suddenly, the flame wavered, flickered, and was snuffed completely out. The pit was full of carbonic acid gas. Then the crowd fully conceived the terrible thing that had happened to those three men, and a great groan of dismay arose. Each man as he had descended into the pit had been gripped by the gaseous monster lying in wait for him, suffocated without a chance to give a warning cry, and fallen limply off the ladder to the bottom. At the first realisation of the horror Mrs. Ashton- Selby had fainted ; her daughters hysterically cried and shrieked in their terror. Confusion reigned at the head of the well ; the workmen either rushed aimlessly about accomplishing nothing, or else looked dazedly on, staggered by the horror of the catastrophe. All was panic. Then of a sudden the vicar's voice asserted itself above the noise of the screaming women. "Quick," be said, "bind this around me." And he seized the rope attached to the windlass and threw it over his shoulders.</p>
        <p>"But you can't go down there, sir," said one of the men. "It's certain death. Nobody can live a minute in that gas." "Don't question me," he cried. "Do as you're told," and there was the masterly tone of decision in his voice. The crowd drew round, tremulously expectant. "Now dip ibis handkerchief in water. I'm going into this well, boys," lie said. "'Watch me closely until I have passed the platform. Then if you feel the weight of my body on this lope you will know it has been too much for me. You can haul up then, but not a second before. You understand ? " He dropped quickly down the ladder until he reached the platform. Then taking a long breath, he tied the handkerchief tightly about lits mouth and hurriedly stepped into the pit of death. A silence as of the grave took possession of all that crowd upon the downside. Suddenly there was a spasmodic twitch at the rope, and those holding it knew what it meant, and began hauling up as fast as they could. Who rove was tent, but not</p>
        <p>neavy, ana mair nopts ran nign. as t!sy cranst their heads over the well's mouth, a hana call% in view, then the vicar's head. In a trice more it wai seen that he had with him a burden, and, impatient to help, three or four men clambered down to the platform, and, seizing their pastor bodily, pulled him and the human burden he carried out upon the boards. The rescued man WBS Ashton-Selby, the vicar's enemy and opponent. A loud cheer broke from the throats of those nearest the edge of the pit. It was taken up by everyone, even the Bishop, and the ladies helped to swell the sound.</p>
        <p>But the vicar's work was not yet completed. Leaving the engineer lying a heavy, apparently lifeless mass upon the platform, he once more 9ssayed an entry into the poisonous chamber. He gas not gone so long this time, when once more he appeared bearing an inanimate body in his arms. Yet again, despite the entreaties of the people, did he face death. He found the third man as he had found the other two, lying prone at the foot of the ladder, and brought him to the surface. Then, utterly spent, with the cheers of the crowd echoing in his fast deafening ears, the vicar fell prostrate upon the planking.</p>
        <p>Two men only survived that terrible immersion in the deathly gas, Ashton-Selby and his rescuer, the vicar. The contractor was the first to recover, and the vicar's life is,credited to the special advice summoned from London by his erstwhile enemy. Exactly what happened between the contractor and the vicar, when at length Asht,on-Selby was permitted by the doctors to see their clerical patient, is not quite known, but the big man was seen leaving the Vicarage with a thoughtful face, and he never was known to recount the story of the vicar's rescue without a tear somehow forcing its way to his eyes. . . .</p>
        <p>Mrs-. Ashton-Selby and her daughters are the most constant visitors Mrs. Robert Stone can claim on her list, and the latest excitement in Saffron Redley is an agitation set on foot by the ex-wife- beater. Dicky Tansom. for increased sitting accom- modation in the mural ror the poor, the welt-to-do people having crowded almost everybody else out. Vicar Stone is still following up the squire's recommendation, and keeping on with the conver- sion of the parish on his own lines. The One Mile basis has answered.</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Rev. G. Harbottle's Visit. Sermons and Lecture at Bewsey-road. The Rev. George Harbottle, who at one time worked in the Warrington Wesleyan Methodist circuit,is always a welcome visitor to the town and on Sunday large congregations assembled at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church when he preached at the Wesley Guild festival. At the morning service Mr. Harbottle spoke eloquently on " Large-hearted religion," his text being " I will run the way of thy commandments, when thou shalt enlarge my heart" (Psalm cxix. v. 32). Alluding to hindrances to the spread of religion, he said many reasons were advanced. One man said drink was the hindrance, another the materialism of the age, and another the new theology. He (the preacher) would undertake to say that one of the greatest hindrances to the acceptance of religion by the man outside was the poor manner in which Christian people themselves represented and expressed their religion, so that often- times one was tempted to think that one met with a larger type of man outside the Church than within it. Men in the Church concerned themselves rather with small things than with the great things of religion and if they would only present to the world an expression of a large-hearted religion they would do much to commend that religion to those outside. Miss Emily Taylor sweetly sang " Entreat me not to leave thew" In the afternoon there was a musical service, presided over by Dr. James Bennett, of IPadgate. The Warrington Mile Voice Choir was ins, I attendance and gave effective interpretations of " The phantom host," " Son of God," Destruction of Gaza " I and " The Crusaders," Mr. Hassan ably conducting. Mr. IW. Clay feelingly sang "The Lost Chord " an 1 Mr. [Booth was heard to advantage in " The psalm divine." I In the evening Mr. Harbottle took for hie teet " This 1 is a faithful saying and worthy of all acceptation, that ! Jesus Christ came into the world to save sinners." I He said the words "Jesus Christ came into the world" were a simple expression which might be used of anybody, but when they used it of Jesus Christ it implied pre-existence. It I was difficult to-day for men to believe in the I supernatural, and therefore many honest men, knowing ' well the great service that Christianity had rendered to ! mankind, were disposed to drop the supernatural element. They thought that if that element were shed, , Christianity had yet a great future baron) it. The , preacher said it might be granted that these men were sincere and honest minded, but they were mistaken. If Christianity could be explained in the term, of natural , evolution, if man could explain it, he could have made , it. But his contention was that Christianity was some- thing that man could not have done for himself, but was done for hi.n by God. The mission of Christ was a mission from the other side. The great gulf ' between the visible and the invisible had been bridged , and the everlasting silence had been broken in Christ, ; and it was because Christianity had supernatural I elements in it that it had commended itself to the , hearts of mankind. The duet" Love divine " was well rendered by Miss F. Bennett and Mr. C. Sinclair and I Miss Hipkiss expressively sang "Lead kindly light." At the close of the service an enjoyable organ recital was given by Mr. W. T. Pearce who ably presided at the instrument throughout the day. "LIFE'S ALMANAC: THINGS SEEN BY THE WAY." On Monday evening there was a good attendance to listen to a lecture by the Rev. G. Harbottle on " Life's Almanac : Things seen by the way," I Alderman TiseeroN, who presided, said the objects of the Wesley Guild had his great sympathy. He was not I sure that Methodism generally recognized the very important link between the Sunday school and the I Church which the guild supplied. It enabled the young I people to join together in fellowship for their common good and strengthened their moral and spiritual char- acter, as well as bringing out social qualities. Alder- man Tinnion concluded by extending a hearty welcome to Mr. Harbottle who, he said, was one of those large- hearted men who seemed to see things in a somewhat broader light than many men and had a happy way of describing them differently. The Rev. G.- HARBOTTLE "dealt with his subject in a highly interesting way, the plan which he followed being to take a fe.v of the leading dates in his own life and see what was happening in the world at those particular periods. The germ of the idea was suggested by a passage in Carlyle's Oliver Cromwell "in which he pointed out that on the same day that Cromwell was entering a College at Cambridge—April 23rd, 1613 —Shakespeare was taking his farewell of life. He (Mr. Harbottle)made his arrival in the world in the year 1819 , and he had found that that year was a very remarkable one. Lord Macaulay, speaking on his re-election in , Edinburgh in 1852 of the preceding five years,said: "Never since the origin of our race have there been five years more fertile of great events, five years which have left , I behind them amore awful lesson. The revolutions of ages I have been compressed into a few months. The streets I of the greatest capitals of Europe were piled up with barricades and were streaming with civil blood. The house of Orleans fled from France. The Pope fled from Rome. The Emperor of Austria was not safe in Vienna. One Republican Convention was sitting in the city of Berlin and another Republican Convention was sitting in the city of Frankfort." The year 1849 witnessed one of the most disastrous agitations that had ever shaken the Methodist church, an agitation that shook it from centre to circumference. The embers of that fierce controversy were scarcely yet cooled and the wisest men would hesitate to apportion fairly the praise and blame for an agitation which cost their church 100,000 of its members. More interesting to him was the fact that in 1819 the poet Wordsworth was sinking quietly to his rest, but another star of equal magnitude and perhaps of greater brilliancy had arisen for in that year Tennyson was passing through the Press +he proof sheets of his great poem " In Memoriam." That year constituted the most wonder- ful of Tennyson's life for he came into the laureateship in succession to Wordsworth. Passing on to his school days, Mr. Harbottle referred to the Crimean war and to the anxiety with which news of the battles was awaited in his native village. On all sides to-day the Crimean war was regarded as having been a mistake. John 1, Bright, to his honour, thundered against it throughout ' his life and Lord Salisbury, a little while before his death, said we put our money on the wrong horse on that occasion. Mr.Harbottle gave graphic descriptions of the " Soldiers' battle at Inkermann and the charge of the Light Brigade. Scarcely had the echo of the guns at Sebastopol died away before we woke up a second time to more serious and ominous strife. It was scarcely possible for those who were not living at the time to realize how deeply the feelings of the people of England were stirred by the horrors of the Indian Mutiny. Throughout Europe it seem ad as if the great , Indian empire founded by Clive, consolidated by the genius of Hastings, and justified if ever Empire was by the most just and beneficent rule of a subject people the world had ever seen, was slipping from our grasp. Passing on from the Indian Nlntiny to revolutions of , thought, Mr. Harbottle pointed out that it was in 1858 that Darwin who had been long gathering the facts that were to establish the law of evolution and natural selection, found that Russell Wallace had anticipated his great discovery. It was wonderful to think that those two great men labouring far apart, working each on his own lines, had stumbled upon the most revolu- tionary doctrine that had ever been promulgated. Since the time that Galileo established that the sun was the centre of our system there had not been any discovery comparable in importance to the establishment of natural selection and the doctrine of evolution by Darwin. Fifty years had passed and we were all revolutionists now, and thank God we were more hi stian believers than we ever were. (Applause.) Mr.</p>
        <p>Harbottle alluded to the two great wars of liberation— the civil war in America which (mired great suffering in this country and particularly in harm 'shire and to the conflict to liberate Italy, He eulogised Stonewall Jackson and Garabaldi as the p milar hr ea of these struggles. There were,hesaicdpeopls to-day who dreamed about the restoration of the temporal power of the Pope and others dreamed of the restoration of the Stuart ' Dynasty in England. He hoped the one was as likely to come about as the other. It was about the period to which he was alluding that Tnackeray died. In 1819 he was publishing " Vanity Pair " in serial form and it received a questionable acceptance. It was now regarded as his greatest work and a book worthy to rank with the finest pieces of English fiction. To an ardent yoimg Methodist one of the great events in life was one's first sight of the Wesleyan Conference and Mr. Harbottle gave some interesting reminiscence of his first Confer- ence in 1873, when Luke Wiseman,the retiring President, delivered a most eloquent charge defining the Wesleyan Methodist position in reply to a pastoral by Bishop Wordsworth of Lincoln. With regard to the future, Mr. Harbottle said we were living in one of the most extraordinary times of English history and when anyone sixty years hence came to compile an almanac of life he would have to tell of sonie wonderful things and of things which meant pro- gress. Tbere were plenty of men ready to prognisti- ' cats evil and to prophesy that the future would be black with storms, disaster and revolution. He CMG Harbottle) was not one of them, and he would apply to the present outlook the words uttered by Lord Macaulay in 1849 before the student of Glasgow University:—" Ever since I began to make observation on the state of my country, I have been seeing nothinz but growth and hearing of nothing but decay. The more I contemplate our noble institutions, the more convinced I am that they are sound at heart, that they have nothing of age but its dignity, and that their strength is still the strength of youth. They still stand, august and immovable, while dynasties and churches tre lying in ruins all around us. Ise no reason to doubt that by the blessing of God on a wise and temperate policy, on C policy of which the principle is to preserve what is • good by reforming in time what is evil, our civil institutions may be preserved to a late posterity." (Applause). On the motion of Mr. mrrcuELL, seconded by Mr. E. J. GLT EST a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer, chairman, and to the organist and choirmaster for their val able services during the festival.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>" Love Rules the World." A Sermon on a Play-Bill. The Rev. John Yonge and the Signs of the Times.</head>
        <p>" Love Rules the World." A Sermon on a Play-Bill. The Rev. John Yonge and the Signs of the Times.</p>
        <p>" Love Rules the World "was the subject of the Rev.. John Yonge's aerators at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening' and he took for his text the words : "The greatest of these is Love " (1 Cor. xiii.' pt. 13). He- said he did not make any apology for finding a subject for a sermon on a play-bill exhibited on the hoardings of the town and advertised in conspicuous characters in the local Press. " Love Rules the World "—the title' was so striking and attractive. It was a title which semehow challenged one. Did love rule the world? had it ever done so ? Was it doing so to-day ? When was it going to be true ? It was a challenging sort of expectally just now. It first met their gaze at the , end of last week and then it received last Sunday night the bewildering commentary of the tragedy at Lisbon ! ' When the regicides in Portugal rushed upon King Carlos land shot him and his son to instant death, it hardly ; looked as if love ruled the world. When alsp peaceful gatherings of people, met for the worship of the God who was love, were suddenly invaded by telegraphic news of that dastardly and diabolical assassination, the , feeling instantly created was contradictory of ! the rule of love. The very opposite appeared ;to be demonstrated, beyond all denial. Not yet, at any rate, had love reached its destined supremacy in the world. Their Christian belief, how- ever, most assuredly was that in spite of all contra- dictory appearances, love did rule the world, because ;they believed and were sure that God reigned supreme ! over all the discords and disturbances of human life. " God is Light " and " God is love." Therefore it would come to pass at length that the evil works of darkness would come to an end and that love would prevail. It would bring into harmony the most divided I and disorganized sons of men, and then the reign of love and peace would be established for evermore. The preacher then emphasized the fact that love ought to ' dominate home life, and also that when God had given ' them the delights of love, he had laid upon them a I mission to promote those delights, to make them ; possible for all around thorn, te do nothing which could ever make others unhappy, which could go to rob hearts lof love and homes of peace. We were living to-day in the most hopeful age for the human race which the world had ever known. The spirit of broad and generous humanity was awakening in the Church of Christ. In days past the great and sovereign ' idea in the Church had been personal salvation, ; the salvation of the soul. Nor need they, even i now, deem that to be of less than incalculable import- ance. But the great spirit of the Saviour of men had been opening the eyes and hearts of His disciples to , see and feel the necessity of saving their fellows even. 1 from the temporal want and suffering which were so prevalent in the world. The Christian conscience had ; been aroused to the sense of duty in the service of ' humanity. We were now being confronted, constantly and variously and urgently, with social claims, social needs, and social sufferings. They appealed to Us in every newspaper ; they were recognized and discussed by all schools of philosophy, politics and religion. All churches and creeds were waking up to the absolute necessity of care and love for the neighbour, the fellow-man or woman who might he in suffering,sorrow. hunger, nakedness and homelessness. This humanitY theme was the topic of the day. The mitigation of poverty, the prevention of unemployment, the suppres- ; sion of sweating, the sanitation of dwellings,the regula- tion of the hours and remuneration of toil, the promo- tion of popular education, the reconciliation of capital and labour,the prevention of strikes and look-outs—these were the matters prevalent and paramount now, and the Church of Christ was called to play its part in their true adjustment. Humanity was appealing to Christianity, and that appeal could not possibly be in vain. "Love rules the world "? It ever it was to do so, the prime mover in the blessed reform must be the Chgrch of the Redeemer, Lie wanted to emphasise the essential Christianity of all the great social movements and programmes of the present day. It was a most hopeful sign of the times that the leaders in all churches were so unanimously espousing the universal welfare of all classes. " God is love " and He was behind all the upheavings of thought, of endeavour, even of combination, political and social, which had in view human betterment. It might be, perhaps it must be that agitation for this great ultimate end would be accompanied by collision of opinion, divergence of method, creating misunderstandings and friction, and even str:fe. But he suggested that the aim and effort of the followers of Jesus of Nazareth should be to bear and forbear in this inevitable controversy. The rule of this great social conflict, which was spreading rapidly, even in other lands than ours, and promised to become oven wide as the world, the true, supreme principle of this movement must be that of human brotherhood. What a line illustration of the influence of a wise, judicious loving kindness had this nation had just lately in the splendid inter- vention of Mr. Lloyd George for the adjustment of trade and labour dialculties. The threatened railway strike and lock-out in the cotton trade found terminations infinitely different from what would have , been likely twenty years ago. It was to be hoped that similar adjustment might be found practicable in the I present dispute in the north, where some 50,000 ship- wrights were on the eve either of strike or lock-out. I Yes, love was to rule the world, but that did not mean any mere tender sentiment; it meant a manly, intelligent, reasonable recognition of mutual rights and necessities, and of the common brotherhood of men. And was not this the very principle and policy for which the times were calling ? We wertaliving in most , critical and in some lands at any rate, most perilous times. Every day we read of "kingdoms and empires i trembling, from Sovereigns to peasants,from palaces to , hovels, with the agitation of fear. The tragedy in Lisbon was but a symptom, after all, of a condition of , society which was not confined, by any means, to that ; small kingdom. We were shocked by that occurrence. i Our indignation swiftly kindled against the assassins- , and their dastard deed. We could not find any conceiv- I able justification of it. But there might have been probably explanation of it. It probably was the mit- t come of an ignorant resentment of a social and national . condition that had become intolerable. When men could gain no redress against oppression they were apt to strike for freedom in ways which reason and right could never sanction. We grieved for the men struck down and for the anguish so created. We sorrowed for the people of Portugal in whose midst the spirit of rebellion and anarchy appeared to have so ; madly uprisen. But, after all, the event might work for a wider good than even the cloaperate evil of its doing. It might teach other monarchs and other 1 governments lessons of humanity which they needed to 5 learn. It might teach the folly and wrong of all r popular oppressions and injustices, the sin of trying to kill the freedom of men, and the supreme necessity of ever governing in the spirit of brotherhood, the spirit 5 of humanity which was the spirit of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>IS YOUR THROAT SORE?</head>
        <p>IS YOUR THROAT SORE?</p>
        <p>A sore or husky throat is often a 'sign of corning tronbie in the bronchial tubes and lungs and no one can afford to neglect it.</p>
        <p>The only Siire-re-Medy is a prompt course of Peps, the unrivalled breathing cure. Place a Pep in the mouth, and the healing, strengthening Peps fumes immediately reach the furthermost limits of the throat, voice-box and bronchial tubes. The worst soreness or inflamma- tion is at once relieved and comforted, and continuing the Peps treatment the soreness, hoarseness, huskiness. or inflammation is removed and the voice completely restored. The throat's resisting power against further attacks is also incomparably strengthened. When buying Peps see the registered title—Peps—on every Is. lid. and 2s. W. box.</p>
