      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen attend Memorial Services.— Ile attendance of the King and Queen at the Requiem Mass, and also at the memorial service in St. Paul's Cathedral, for the late King and Crown Prince of Portugal, was a fitting expression of the nation's sympathy, with Queen Ardelie and her family, and of their Majesties' horror at the awful tragedy enacted at Lisbon. It is many Tears since King Edward attended service in a Roman Catholic Church. There is none of</p>
        <p>' -the bigoted feeling now, which, in former times would ' have prevented the Sovereign from attending the , celebration of a Requiem Mass, but that such an act is unusual, if not unprecedented, shows how deeply the King and Royal Family desired to express their sympathies with the bereaved in Lisbon. The requiem service was celebrated in the Church of St. James' where King Alfonso and his Queen attended Mass on several occasions, during their recent visit to London. The celebration in St. Paul's Cathedral was marked by the entire absence of Royal State. It was attended by a large congregation, everyone present wearing mourn- ing, and was a very solemn and impressive affair. . Court Mourning.—The Court remains in mourning for • the late King and Crown Prince of Portugal until March 2nd, causing the postponement of several State functions. That was inevitable under the circumstances but, in keeping with modern views on such matters, Court mourning is not the lengthy or sombre affair it Used to be. In former times the offiCials of the Court • seemed to take an almost ghoulish delight in ordering 'general mourning " which was regarded with some ' satisfaction by the journeymen tailors, for whom it • meant double wages' but other trades suffered and , petitioned Paliament against the practice of ordering ' general mourning,"—when this appeared to go beyond the necessity of the occasion. People in olden times ~ took a dismal delight in the parade and display of the 'trappings of woe ' but in these days we more sensibly desire to minimise these outward exhibitions as much as Possible, and with individual exceptions here and there, the change has extended more or less to all •, •classes of people. A Glance at Lisbon.—Undoubtedly the most striking find imposing buildings in Lisbon are its vast and • Massive convents, which crowd the succession Achille nPon which the capital of Portugal is built. Next come •', the Churches, tho Cathedral being a Romanesque edifice 'fine, the twelfth century, small and not particularly the greatest interest in ecclesiastical buildings being centred in the exquisite convent and church of riti Geronimo, at Belem, a suburb of Lisbon. The building is a mixture of the classic and flamboyant • styles, and about four hundred years old. Other fine 'churches are the Carmo, greatly damaged in the earth- quake of 1755, never repaired, but full of interest ; 'and St. Vicerlte de Fora, to which the Royal Pantheon is attached—large, lofty, and imposing,and where rest the Mortal remains of the late genial King and his young ' heir. There are several new palaces, including the new Royal Palace, a magnificent edifice completed some - "forty years ago, the Ajunda, on a bill overlooking Belem, two small palaces in Belem, the Bemposta,north of the city, now converted into a military school, and the palace of the Cortes of the legislative chambers. Lisbon has frequently been visited by earthquakes, but ' 'all these have been nothing as compared with the great earthquake of 1755, when some 60,000 persons perished, ~ and the loss of property was reckoned at £20,000,000 Stirling. The climate is variable, but on the whole ' healthy, and just now, with spring commencing, the ' , 'Weather is glorious. ,- Fire at Windsor Castle.—The slight outbreak of fire St Windsor Castle the other day, recalls the fact that Queen Victoria was once alarmed by a fire at the Castle. ' On March 19, 1853, she had, with Prince Albert arrived I at the Castle to spend the Easter holidays, and shortly . after dinner, intimation was received that l'rinces ler was on fire. Queen Victoria was very much , anZ,tened at first, and she and her ladies adjourned to Part"el Part of the Cagle and remained up the greater , ?th, °I the night. Writing two days afterwards to , an'"aa Leopold, she said, "It was a serious affair, such I sa,.augnaintance with what a fire is, and with its Deus- Th:3', accompaniments, does not pass from one's mind &amp;Ictinout leaving a deep impression." Queen Alexandra 0,:, an even more alarming experience when afire broke , s" in the middle of the night in her bedroom at andringliam, some two or three years ago. M t. a-a I ng better use of the Daylight.—lt is the practice 11 Many households to start the day earlier in the prnmer than in the winter. None can doubt that the araily benefits by such an arrangement and there is a good deal to be said for the proposal that we should make better use than we do of the daylight portion of ,ae twenty-four hours in the summer mouths. Mr. r earce has proposed to effect this by introducing a Bill ' In the House of Commons, which would require all clocks to be advanced-20, minutes on each of four undays in Anril,and the process reversed in September. 2:9llsequently from May to August although we might . Still breakfast at nine by the clock, the clay would only 8 advanced as far as 7.40, and midday by the sun Would be 1.20 by tife clock. If it were made legal and ; tlniversal we should still go to bed, breakfast, begin,and the day's work, at the same conventional hours that we do now, the only change would be that at all these periods the solar day would be 80 minutes less . ranced than under the system we are accustomed to , :s.stat would not be good for the gas and lighting cora- .t;ries, it would not be wanted so much in Scotland . B,!e, in June. daylight extends till nearly midnight, n England it would afford the people another hour's a4llght for tennis, croquet, and other outdoor games " recreations, after the day's work was over. GOv ari..f ernment Clerkships for Women.— A query has "en with respect to a recent paragraph in this 1,9,11,8 Th, relating to Civil Service Examinations for lari"n`ln Clerks. It was stated that two languages, Thiac?,' German, or Latin, were compulsory subjects. option'asl not exactly the case. They are nominally for . subjects, but at the same time it is necessary th candidates to take two of these languages, otherwise " could not hope for success, the competition being ,•. ves3' keen. -, ~ l Fashions in Ilings.—Sometimes modish women load ‘ne'r fingers with rings, and sometimes they lay these •. Particular ornaments sternly aside, the plain wedding ling being the only one in evidence. At the present t , .. oMent _rings are greatly to the fore, indeed the Isehionable woman has evidently discovered that her , n'gbt fingers afford insufficient accommodation for all ,Loe gems she desires to exhibit at one time, for she is '?-eking use of her thumbs, and wearing thuinh rings 1-11 ! ,notion is a very ancient one revived, and, calculated 4) ,1O spoil the most beautiful hand. Moreover, the over- Jewelled band is a mistake, and the idea at present in Vogue of wearing lino set with immense gems, espeoi- ally upon the second and fourth fingers, is certainly to he deplored. - Mixing Furs.—At one time it was considered impos- -B_,ible to mix different kinds of laces, or furs, now we , 'rho both, and with tho most charming iesults. Often 1.„,t:r0 and more kinds of lace appear upon one gown, or enaPs, while the mixture of furs is quite a usual pro- "'lure- Ermine and sable is the alliance most sought ' after but this is only, of course, for the favoured few. u..,ne Point to remember is never to mix furs of the ' „,*".me type and colour, such for example as two kinds of „`"'na, or mink and sable, and when the fur sales are : l'rThounced, later in the year, it would be well to bear Mind the fact that slowly hut surely the tide of biv°nr is sweeping in the direction of brown and kir skins and long haired furs. . tlle Tea Aprons.—The pretty fanciful afternoon t;rBni greatly beloved by dear old ladies some five and tilanty years ago, has put in an appearance again. At aq., Moment many a fashionable hostess dons over her of ;Noon gown, a similar dainty creation, usually built aboace, very much berufiled, and caught here and there to_sit the bodice with jewelled pins, in which she does set" fancy work and pours out tea. What with our _aernoon aprons, our bead bags, and our night-caps, •.are now-a-days very old-world, and, it must be "‘anitted, very picturesque too.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
