      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>NEW LINGERIE UNDERSKIRTS.</p>
        <p>In the new lingerie underskirts of batiste, fine lawn, or cambric, the ribbons drawn through beading appear on the edge of the flounce in- stead of the top. On one design in the shops there was a broad insertion of I.Fice which headed the deep flounce, and a smart ruffle of lace appeared at the edge. The ribbon in a pale shade of blue was run through wide slits, cut and button-holed just between the insertion and the ruffle. A large bow of the ribbon fastened on to the top of the lace insertion made a pretty finish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NtiN'S-VEILING LOLNGE GOWN.</head>
        <p>NtiN'S-VEILING LOLNGE GOWN.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a dainty dressing or lounge gown, which would work out very in- expensively in cream nun's-veiling or any pretty colour. The upper part—of the gown, which is short-waisted. is lined as far as the waist, and the very small bodice is gathered into a band at the waist and again into the deep rounded yoke, the long, flowing skirt being left quite plain.</p>
        <p>The nee&amp; a nnislied with a band of deep cream lace insertion and a knot of some 'bright- coloured ribbon. The yoke is also outlined with the insertion, which is arranged to hang down the front in short stole ends. There are loose- hanging bands of insertion round the top of dui sleeves, and insertion again trims the wide cuffs.</p>
        <p>~.. ' .....;,„. * - 1 .t 5 • -q ' , „• , ! A" , \ rlfil, t pi i 1 t i 1 • ' : 1 I.; . [ j'fil• -- V_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>HAIRDRESSING.</head>
        <p>HAIRDRESSING.</p>
        <p>There has seldom been a period when women had so great an opportunity of adding to their charms as now in the matter of hairdressing, says the Queen. Look round the beautiful winter exhibition of Old Masters at the Academy, at the Romneys there, the women in Hogarth's pic- tures. the Sir Peter Lelys, and the rest, and you will see that almost all the styles that were worn then, when, it would seem, almost every woman had a charce of looking beautiful, and certainly of looking her best, are not at all out of keeping with the dress fashions of the day or of the cur- rent modes in hairdressing. One great advantage women have now is that the coiffeurs of the day have mastered the difficulties of keeping our tresses in good condition. When a clever hair- dresser arranges your hair he manages by his deft way of combing and brushing for it to come well apart, and to be light and easy to dress, and it is a thousand pities some people do not have their ladies'-ma ids taught this art, and learn how to comb and brush their hair themselves as the best hairdressers do it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>UNIQUE PERSONALITIES.</head>
        <p>UNIQUE PERSONALITIES.</p>
        <p>The cultivation of a unique personality has be- come so much a vogue that it is now quite 00111M0 nplace to look uncommon, remarks a writer in the Tatter. As a girl who was once quite remarkable for her artistic taste in dress said to me the other day, "What is the use of being picturesque when everybody you meet is too?" This girl, by the way, has taken to the severest tailor clothes in disgust. But from any way you look at it it certainly becomes harder every year for anyone with ambition to stand out from among the herd of her fellow-women with any kind of originality. And it is much harder work for the originators of fashions and the dresmakers who clothe us. Instead (as was once the simple method of ordering a gown) of the dressmaker shewing boldly a frock and by the statement, "This is what I am just making for Lady So-and-so," eliciting scores of orders for another exactly like it, each article we wear has to be ushered into the world with the most ex- treme mystery. The modern dressmaker has a consulting-room of even more profound privacy than that of the most learned physician.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FITTING BODICE.-</head>
        <p>FITTING BODICE.-</p>
        <p>, • Get this into your mind</p>
        <p>In our sketch is shewn a pretty bodice which can be made from quite short lengths of material like the skirt, with the help of remnants of lace and silk. The bodice is made on a fitted lining, so that the over-bodice, which is af material like the skirt, can be nicely and firmly draped. It is</p>
        <p>tucked on the shoulders and bordered with darker-toned velvet. The - fronts meet at the yoke, but are drawn away to the sides at the waist to show a vest of silk and little waistcoat- like lapels of lace. There is also a band of lace attached beneath the edge of the wide armholes. The yoke and sleeves are of silk, the yoke being tucsed, and the gigot-shaped sleeves are rucked sound the arm.</p>
        <p>4 a :;•,,,,"' . 1.,- „7..1:::---- r,,'. ~...4 . ' t , -4.1 z' Lc. ' ' • ,i.. , la,-. '''!-. figin ,»- i 41, ( 4. ‘ \je • ( , 'C. , ; _ '' ' ' e, (• ..) ;,-..i,,/ i , /,- , ; / / / i ,11, \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>"Bovril repels Influenza"</head>
        <p>"Bovril repels Influenza"</p>
        <p>T!, 7i=mcv,</p>
        <p>WOMEN IN THE PUBLIC SPHERE.</p>
        <p>It will be noticed, writes "Lady Phyllis" in the Bystander, that, as a rule, excepting, of course, if there is any particular impulse of genius, when women seek public sphere of action or entrance to one or other of the professions it is from necessity rather than ambition. This necessity, however, is growing, and begins to present a serious problem both to parents and young people, for with the increased number of openings for girls there is likewise an increased and ever-increasing army of women workers. The great exhibition in connection with the Girls' Bealin Guild of Service, which is to be held in London this year, ought to be of valuable assistance to many seeking a profession. All possible information will be obtainable, while the demonstrations should be of interest to those to whom artistic labour is a joy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>SAILOR SUITS FOR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>SAILOR SUITS FOR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The popularity of the sailor suit for both boys and girls is ever on the increase. A charming variation of the mode, says our Paris correspond- ent, consists of a blouse that may be slipped on over the head, or closed in front, with wide shoulders, and having the back extending in a shallow yoke, a sailor collar, and sleeves in bishop or sailor style; and a five-gored, side- pleated skirt, with the pleats stitched to yoke depth or left free. In dark blue serge, with trimmings of red braid, the effect would be very smart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>SAVOURY PANCAKES.—Put six tablespoonfuls of flour in a basin with a teaspoonful of salt, make this into a batter of the proper consistency, as thick as double cream, with three large eggs, well beaten, and a little milk; beat this mixture briskly with a wooden spoon till every lump is smooth, then stir in a tablespoonful of minced onion, a teaspoonful of minced herbs, and a good dust of Deoner. Let the batter stand a few hours. men try a small teacupful at a time in bolting tat or lard. As each pancake is finished, roll it up bolster fashion, sprinkle pepper and salt over, and serve as quickly after being cooked as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Anwmic—not Lazy.</head>
        <p>Anwmic—not Lazy.</p>
        <p>The Weary Languid Existence. of a</p>
        <p>Bloodless -Girl rendered Bearable and Bright again by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>Unjustly accused of being lazy erhile really wailer- ing from illness was the predicament in which Miss Hetty Ambler, of 2, Bell Street, Halifax, found herself. This young lady was Antetnic, and the deadly languor of that complaint, regarded as laziness by Miss Ambler's healthy friends, was actually a sign that she was devoid of strength and vitality. How she found health through Dr. Williams' Pink Pills and disposed of the accusation is a story that makes instructive reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>STRANGE LANGUOR AND WEARINESS.</head>
        <p>STRANGE LANGUOR AND WEARINESS.</p>
        <p>" I failed to understand the strange weariness which came over me in my early teens," began Miss Ambler thoughtfully, " because I could not point to any particular pain or ailment. Friends called it ' laziness !' But in time though, they saw their mis- take, and admitted that I was in the grip</p>
        <p>of Anmmia. For—, as time _went on, I</p>
        <p>wasted away 'and grew weaker daily. I suffered from fright- ful headaches and</p>
        <p>racking pains across my back, and before long I had lost strength to such an</p>
        <p>exten'i that only with</p>
        <p>the ,greatest difficulty</p>
        <p>could I crawl about.</p>
        <p>My appetite deserted " tramp over the hil4 me, and I rarely took quite beyond me." more than a cup of _ __ _</p>
        <p>tweak tea at meal-times. Now I dreaded the slightest movement, and though once I enjoyed a good tramp over the Halifax hills this was now beyond me. My heart palpitated cruelly.</p>
        <p>HER DIGESTION ALL AT FAULT.</p>
        <p>"My breathing grew hard and painful ; I could digest no solid food, not even bread and butter ; for all food caused excruciating pain across my chest. I gradually grew worse, and a numbness began to creep over me ; my hands and feet alike felt cold and lifeless, and my nerves were thoroughly disordered."</p>
        <p>" And how vere you cured ?" the reporter asked. " I had lost all interest in life,' replied Miss Ambler, " when a dear friend told me that my Anzemia would vanish if I took Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. So I sent for a supply of the pills and took them. Before I had finished the first box I knew that my friend's advice was good. It was such a joy to feel energy coming back. THE REWARD OF PERSEVERANCE.</p>
        <p>" I continued taking the pills, and so lost those awful headaches, and my back grew stronger. I could eat heartily without fear of indigestion. I rested well at night, and rose fresh and ready for work,while breathlessness and palpitation troubled me no more, My nerves became. steady, and I had a clear and brilliant complexion once more. Now I never exper- ience any symptoms of Anaemia, Indigestion, or Weak- ness, and for this I thank Dr. Williams' Pink Pills." The peculiar languor of pernicious Anaemia led t 3 Miss Hetty Ambler being most unjustly labelled " Lazy " by her fziends. Too often Antrmia is neglected and allowed to develop into the early stages of consumption. Dr. Williams Pink Pills supply rich, good blood to the starved veins, and so have cured Anicmia, Bloodlessness, Heart Palpitation, Hysteria, Indigestion, Nervous Disorders, Neuras- thenia, end also the ailments that afflict women only. Only the genuine pills, those labelled in full, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People effect these cures. Of all dealers, or send 2s. 9d. for one box' or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, direct to the British Depot, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. Substitutes are useless.</p>
