      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—Ou Monday evening in the Wesleyan Schoolroom an interesting lecture was given under the anspices of the above Guild, by Mr. Anderson, of Manchester on" The Awakening of China." The Rev.W. Mellor occupied the chair and there was a large attendance of members. The lecturer showed that he had a very good grasp of Chinese affairs and read extrPlts from letters from one of his brothers a medical gentleman who is resident in China. The lecture was intnspersed with descriptions of Chinese life and customs, and it was demonstrated that there was a slow but sure change taking place in favour of Western ideals and practices. Recently, however, the English and German officials and teachers were being displaced by Japanese and as they were mainly atheistical in their views, a real danger in the future might arise in cense- nuence and the lecturer appealed to his hearers to assist Missionary efforts for the Christianising of China. At the close of the lecture a vote of thanks was on the proposition Mr. B. Mason, seconded by Mr. E. W. Plinston passed to Mr. Anderson for his interesting and profitable address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ANNUAL LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The a ''''''' Newton sessional District were held at Newton on Thursday. The district comprises Earlestown, Newton, Golborne, Haydock and 13urtonwood. Reports Rs to licensing affairs in these places were submitted. The Chairman (Captain withington) said the reports core most satis- factory. The liceused victuallers seemed to have con- ducted their houses in an exemplary manner. There were no applications for new licennes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 15, 1908</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</head>
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>Annual Dinner.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner and social in connection with Court " Warrington 430 " was held at the Blue Lion inn on Saturday evening last, D.C.R. Bro. Walter Smith presiding, supported by S.D.C.R. Bro. Walsh. District, Trustee, District Treasurer Bro. Peter Peacock, P.D.C.R.'s Bros. Chrimes, James, Rotherham, Hankin- I son, Trustee Bro Gregory, C.R. Bro. Fowler, and P.C.R.'s Bros. Clayton and Sam Healey. Over eighty sat down to a substantial repast, which the members did full justice to, and the host, Mr. Lake, was com- plimented on the excellent manner in which the catering I was arranged. The CHAIRMAN, District Chief Ranger: Bro. Walter Smith, in his address to the members, in a few well- chosen remarks, encouraged them with a review of the history of the Ancient Order of Foresters from its com- mencement over one hundred years 11,,0, pointing out that the membership had risen since 189 from 047,000 to 921,000 at the end of last year. The funds at the latter date amounted to 83 millions, notwithstanding the payment for sick and funeral benefits amounting to over £900,000 per annum. P.D.C.R. Bro PETER PEACOCK' dealing with the toast of the %Carrington District, explained that in \Carrington the Courts had at the end of 1907 £3,849, being an increase for the year of £165 in the district funeral funds, in addition to this they had £2,772 in the sick funds, notwithstanding £1,104 being paid out last year for sick and funeral benefits alone. He compli- mented the District upon the continued success, numerically and financially, and he hoped that the steady progressive policy, which is adopted, will be the means of further urging Forestery in Warrington District. _ I</p>
        <p>P.D. C. R. Bro. CITRIIIES, the esteemed secretary of the Court, dealt with the toast of Court 430, giving a review of same from the year 1835, when they had a membership of 70, and funds, £B3, whereas to-day there are 381 members with funds £1t547 ; during this period he stated £7,369 had been paid in sickness and funeral benefits alone. Dealing however with the 12 years during which he has been secretary, there was an increase in funds of .£477,and 142 members. During this time special relief to distressed members,in addition to 1 sick and funeral beneflts,has been paid amounting to £123. With such a record as this, he emphasised particularly upon all good Foresters the instability of tontines and dividing clubs. He warned the members to steer clear of all such organizations, which were under no state control or actuarial valuations, and asked them to stick to the order, which had a reputation to main- tain and was under the protection of the Registrar General. (Applause.) During the evening a splendid marble time piece was presented to P.D.C.R. Bro.W.Hankinson,for his services as Treasurer for the last 12 years. Also a presentation was made to Bro. Ernest Clayton, for being Chairman for two terms. A presentation of an umbrella aud bag was made to P.C.R. Bro. Roden, for bringing the most members. The social programme was contributed by several of the brethren, and the members particularly enjoyed the songs, etc., rendered by G. Bennett, W. Martin and Mr. Stout. .</p>
        <p>The evening was one of the most enjoyable which had been held.</p>
        <p>Court " Jaaziel " No 138.</p>
        <p>A supper and social at which over 40 members and friends were present, took place at the " White Bull " Hotel on Tuesday last in connection with Court " Jaaziel " No. 138 of this Order, and waa a great success. The supper was followed by a smoking concert daring which a very excellent programme was gone through. The chair was occupied by D.C.R. Bro. W. R. Smith who opened the proceedings by an interesting address regarding the" Order " generally and this Court in particular. He pointed out that for some time past the officers of this Court had been working hard to make it a successful one and be was glad to say that they had apparently succeeded in their efforts. Although No. 138 was not financially strong be hoped before long to find it one of the best in the district.</p>
        <p>The toast of "His Majesty The King and Royal Family" having been submitted by the chairman and acknowledged with musical honours, the first item on the programme, a pianoforte solo by Bro. R. Harnblett, was given followed by a gramaphone selection by Mr. F. Falshaw, and a song by Mr. W. Beswick. P.C.R. Bro. W. Rovlance proposed the toast of the "Warrington District " and this was replied to by the Caairman and supported by P.D.C.R. Bro. L. Davies, District Secretary. This was followed by the presentation to P.D.C.R. Bro. W. Hipkiss of a handsome picture of a landscape view, suitably framed and inscribed, which had been subscribml for by the membeh of the Court. The duty of making this presentation devolved upon P.C.R. Bro. J. Powell, who in the course of his remarks said that the picture had hien subscribed for by the mem- bers of the Court as a small acknowledgment of the valu- able services rendered by Bra. Hipkiss, and who during the many years he had held the office of Court Secretary had done some excellent work in the interest of the Court. He pointed out that it wra mainly on account of the untiring efforts of Bro. Hipkiss that the affairs of the Court were placed on a sound footing.—The remarks of Bro. Powell were ably supported byP.D.C.R. Bro. P. Per lock, and by P.C.R. Bro. J. Humphries.— Bro. Hipkiss in his reply thanked the members ot the Court for their kind appreciation of his efforts in pre- senting him with such a beautiful picture. The pre-entation of a framed emblem of office, suit- ably in. ribed, to P.C.R. Bro. E. A. Jones, was `the next item, and by spacial request this was made on bebalf of the Court, by P.D.C.R. Bro. J. Chrimes,rad P.C.R. Bro. T. Basket 'Me seconded the remarks of Bro. Chrimes and Bro. Jones made a suitable reply. The presentation of a framed emblem to P.C.R. Bro. W. Roylance was afterwards made on behalf of the Court, by S.D.C.R. Bro. J. Walsh who made reference to the good work done by Bro. Roylance during the period of his office. At intervals songs were given by Bros. T. Glover, R. Hamblett, W. Jones and Mr. W. Beswick the first named being loudly encored. The toast of " The Visiting Brethren " was proposed by P.C.R. Bro. Jones and seconded by C. R. Bro. Woods, while that of " Tbe Host, the Hostess and Artistes," seconded by C.R. Bro. Powell Pnd seconded by P.D.C.R. Bro. Hipkiss. The proceedings terminated by a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman for the able manner in which he had officiated, every one agreeing that the evening had beeu a most successful one. The arr ingements were in the hands of Bro. S. Healey assisted by a committee who must be congratulated on the result. To the account of these proceedings might be added the fact that recently Bro. W. Gleave was presented with a very handsome testimonial which took the form of a silver badge, in recognition of his long period of office—over seven years—as "Senior Beadle" to the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>TWO. WORKS FIRES IN ONE DAY.</head>
        <p>TWO. WORKS FIRES IN ONE DAY.</p>
        <p>Shortly after two o'clock on Sunday morning the joiners' and electrical fitting shop of Messrs. ftylands' Bros., Dalton Bank, was found to be on fire by some workmen who were working in the adjoining galvanizing de- -trnent. The alarm was at once raised, the works bed being rung to arouse the workmen living close by. In a short time a number of the workmen got the firm's appliancci to work from a powerful pump, keeping the flames, which had already broken through the roof, from spreading. News of the outbreak was sent to the local fire brigade and Superintendent Turner, is full complement of men, and the steam fire engine" Captain "with reels and ladders turned out and soon got to work. The inflammable nature of the contents of the department caused part of the roof to fall in and a stock of joinery work,a quantity of eiectric light fittings and joiners' tools were totally destroyed. The workmen,under the direction of Messrs. Wadsworth and Warhurst, department foremen, did their utmost and had it not been for them, the adjoining workrooms woeld no doubt have been destroyed. After an hour's work the fire was extinguished. At about seven o'clock in the evening of the same day information was received by telephone that a fire had broken out in one of the ranges of buildings of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, Dallam-lane. The fire brigade with the same appliances es before turned out and on their arrival found a builder's storeroom, a one storey building, which was full of ropes and timber and other material, used by the workmen of the repairing department, in flames. A hydrant was Igot to work and the fire was prevented from spreading to the adjoining building and after about a hour's work the flames were extinguished. The contents of the store were destroyed. Great praise is due to the watchman, who did a great deal to decrease the amount of the damage by removing several barrels of tar which were near the sene of the fire. The damage is estimated at between £5O and gefl. The origin of the fires is enveloped in a mystery.</p>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association. A vigorous campaign of "small meetings " for pledge signing, organized by the abov%Association was held by Miss Kershaw last week, and proved most successfal. The operations of the mission included visits to some of the large manufactories in town by Miss Kershaw and members of the British Women's Temperance Association and " Vs." Messrs. Crosfield, Armitage and Rigby, Greening, and the Alliance Box Company, kindly allowed visits and successful brief meetings for their female employe's took piece, while many pledges were taken. Miss Kershaw addressed impressively the sewing class of the Charity Investigation Society, and Bible classes and Mother's meetings connected with St. Peter's, St. Paul's, St. John's and Emmanuel Churches and Silver- street Mission. At the Girls' Club Silver-street a large meeting was held and presided over by Mrs. T. Owen the President of the B.W.T.A. House to house visitation was also carried on, and numerous pledges were taken during the six days' effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS IN</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS IN</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>A " Highly Undesirable " Practice.</p>
        <p>To prevent a cheese from becoming hard and dry, keep it wrapped in a cloth wrung out in In his first annual report on licensing matters the light ale or water, except (luring the short time Chief Constable of Warrington (Mr. Martin Nicholls) daily when it appears on the table. states that the number of licence holders in the borough is 201, a decrease of 2in the number of ale-houses, as Tar on Clothing.—Rub a little lard on to the compared with last year, caused by the closing on corn- spot and allow it to stand an hour or more, till pensation of the Queen's Ho tel, Sankey-street, and the the tar is softened, when it may be washed out Boar's Head, Pig-Hill. The number of licences now in with hot water and soap. Rinse in clean rain- existence in the Borough as compared with the year waters 1898 show a total decrease of 10. The population of the Borough according to the census of 1901 was Cure for Sore Throats.—Well rub the throat 64,242, consequently there exist (on these figures) in Warrington one licensed house to every 31W61 with ordinary paraffin oil before going to bed, inhabitants. With reference to the and tie round a piece of flannel. Do not soak DRUNKENNESS : the flannel. You will find this a simple cure. there is a decrease of three in the number of persons -- proceeded against as compared with the previous year. To Steam Potatoes.—ln steaming potatoes, The figures for the past five years are as follows : put a cloth over them before putting the lid on. 11907, 220 males and 110 females, total 330 ; 1906, 235 They will take much less time to cook, and be 'males and 98 females, total 333 ' • 1905, 238 males and 96 much more mealy than when done in the ordin- females, total 334 ; 1904, 240 males and 92 females, total ary way, 332 ; 1903, 232 males and 76 females, total 308. It will " be noticed from these figures that during the past five To Keep the Pantry Sweet.—A small box of years there has been a steady increase in the number of unslaked lime k