        <p>COMPARATIVE TABLE shows the number of persons proceeded against for drunkenness in twelve northern towns (including Warrington) for the year ended December 31st, 1907 : Number of Persons pro- Persons Popul at ion ceeded against proceeded by Census of per 1,000 of against. 1901. Populatton. Birkenhead Barrow-in-Furness 608 ... 57,586 ... 10.55 St. Helens Preston 956 ... 112,989 ... 846 Bootle Stockport York l5O ... 77,914 ... 577 Wigan Warrington 330 ... 64,242 ... 513 Burnley 472 ... 117,043 ... 486 Huddersfield .... Halifax 235 ... 104,936 ... 2.43 Having regard to the fact that Warrington is situate on the main road between Liverpool and Manchester, I think it is a matter for congratulation that its position is so low down in the ebove table. Assuming the estimated population of 71,000 to be correct, as far as the residents of the Borough are con- cerned, the number of persons proceeded against for drunkenness per 1,000 of the population was 3.04. I think it desirable, says the Chief Constable, to call the attention of the Licensing Justices to the fact that well-founded complaints have been received by the police of</p>
        <p>How to Clean Fur.—lf our purse is a long one, we send our furs to a cleaner's when they are dirty, but the cost of this is too great for small incomes. Instead, with care, we can clean them quite well at home. Spread a clean cloth on a table, and then rub the fur, if brown or black, the reverse way with warm, dry bran. If the colour is white or pale grey, use finely powdered magnesia instead, and you will have clean furs instead of dirty ones.</p>
        <p>The Art of Darning.—Girls of to-day make a very great mistake in putting on one side all the housewifely arts that our grandmothers excelled in, and among these arts one of the most useful to revive is that of darning. How often a dainty handkerchief is thrown aside because the laundress has torn a hole in it ! Or a pair of stockings has gone from bad to worse because they were not seen to at once. Fine darning is a fine art, and should be cultivated with the greatest care because of its utility.</p>
        <p>For the Cook.—When egg-and-bread-crumb- ing fish for frying, a little milk added to the egg makes it go much farther, and the results are quite as good as when egg alone is used.</p>
        <p>A HIGHLY UNDESIRABLI PRACTICE existing, in the granting of credit to workmen employed at some of the works in the Borough. It would appear from enquiries made that a licensee has an account with an individual who has his name inscribed in a book, and the custom is for the individual in question to hand the book to his fellow workmen requiring beer,to take it to the licensee who supplies same entering tke quantity supplied, and receiving payment at the end of the week at the rate of 21d. per pint, whilst the unfortunate workman has to pay 3d. when settling his account with the man taking upon himself the responsibility of pay- ing for all beer supplied in his name. It is known that the owner of this book, through this source, makes a very considerable income, it being alleged that instances have occurred where workmen have had as many as nine or ten pints of beer whilst at work between 6 p.m. and 11 pm. It is evident from this, that unnecessary temptation is put in the way of the wage-earner, who would probably have to go without at least some of the drink consumed by him, were this system not in vogue, and to my mind it is a practice that should receive condemnation from all, either interested in the trade, or otherwise.</p>
        <p>To Keep Lemons.—Lemons can be kept quite a long time if they are placed on a flat surface under inverted glass tumblers. The tumblers must not be moved till the lemons are needed for use.</p>
        <p>Fore the Pantry Shelves.—An excellent plan is to cover these with white or very light oil- cloth. This can be wiped with a damp cloth when dusty or dirty, and always looks delight- fully fresh and cool. It is quite cheap, so the initial expense is trifling.—" London Journal."</p>
        <p>For Cracked Llps.—Honey and glycerine. mixed in equal proportions is an excellent remedy. Apply it the lak thing at night till the cracks are healed.</p>
        <p>To Whiten the Skin.—lnto half a pint of dis- tilled water put half a wineglassful of fresh- strained lemon-juice. Add a little rose-water tc perfume it, and keep in a tightly-corked bottle. Apply several times a day with a piece of soft linen, allowing it to dry in.</p>
        <p>Poached eggs and onions make a good supper dish. Cut up finely three or four good-sized onions and fry to a light brown in a little pork or beef dripping. Spread this on a deep cli.ll, season with pepper and salt, over all put a layer of fried breadcrumbs, and a few spoonfuls of good gravy. Poach some eggs, lay them on the onions, etc., set in the oven a minute or two, and serve.</p>
        <p>Ham and-Cheese on Toast.--One cup of boiled ham chopped fine, and one-half cup of grated cheese. Mix and .heat. When the cheese IF melted add one .cup cream sauce. Pour over slices of well-browned toast.</p>
        <p>Mutton Steak a la Venison.—Cut slices two- thirds of an inch thick from a leg of mutton. Soak over night or all day in lemon-juice and water (one-harf juice and one-half water). Broil like steak, and pour over sauce made of melted butter and lemon-juice.</p>
        <p>Egg Toast.—Toast round slices of bread; butter and moisten slightly with hot milk; one egg for each slice, Beat the white stiff, form an outer edge of toast, drop the yolk in centre, a dot of butter, sprinkle of salt and white pepper an each yolk. The toast Should be placed in a pan before eggs are added, and put into a good oven until yokes are cooked soft.</p>
        <p>To Perfume an Invalid's Room.—Quite the nicest way of doing thia is to drop a little oil of I:nl,M-wood on a hot shovel. It gives a faint, delightful perfume to the room. 'The oil can be bought at any chemises.</p>
        <p>Boiled Chicken.—To stew chicken, cut it at the joints, making two pieces of each lez; cut the breast into halves, and also the back ; von should make eleven pieces in all. Put the dark meat into the bottom of your stewing-pan and the white meat on top; just cover with boiling water; bring quickly to boiling point; boil for five minutes, and push to the back part of the stove, where it cannot again boil, and keep it there until the meat ic tender. A chicken a year old will require one hour to cook; two years old, two hours. If It boils hard it will be tough. When the meat is half done add a tea- spoonful of salt, and when ready to serve, dish the chicken carefully. Pot two tablespoonfuls of butter and two of flour into s saneepan •, mix. Add one pint of the water in which the chicken was stewed—in fact, you will only have about this much at the end of the cooking. Stir until boiling; add a dash of pepper;, take from the fire, and add the yolk of an egg slightly beaten, and strain it over the chicken. Garnish the dish with tiny baked dumplings.</p>
        <p>Prince of Wales Pudding.—BOat half a pound of butter to a cream, with three ounces of caster sugar, add five eggs (one by one). beating each for three minutes. Mix in half a pound of chopped raisins, two ounces of citron cut in strips.., and half a pound of sifted flour. Boil in buttered mould for three hours, and serve. Here is a suitable sauce for this pudding :—Rasp a lemon with lump sugar, using about two ounces of the sugar, crush and put it in a small saucepan with a wnleglassful of sherly and half a gill of brandy. Add a gill of melted butter sauce, and stir well till hut.</p>
        <p>Good Plum Pudding.—Take half a pound of flour, half a pound of breadcrumbe, one pound of suet, one pound of currants, one pound of raisins (stoned and slightly chopped), one pound of sugar. half a pound of candied peel, half a tea,poonful of salt, half a teaspoonful of mixed spice, and eight eggs. Mix the dry ingredients AN and let them stand several hours, then add the eggs one by one, and stir well. If it does not seem moist enough atter beating some minutes add another egg or a little milk. Turn into a well-buttered mould, tie over; boil for ten hours. Serve with sweet sauce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SPECIAL OFFER.</head>
        <p>A SPECIAL OFFER.</p>
        <p>To Users of Fluid Beef and Meat Extracts.</p>
        <p>We invite the readers of the " Warkagton Examiner' to test for themselves the advantages of the new pre- paration for making Beef Tea, Gravies, Soups, etc. called Fluid Ju-Vis. Fluid Jo-Via is Beef strengthened and vastly improved in flavour by the addition of Vegetable Extract. Fluid Ju-Vis is solely a Beef and Vegetable product and contains no adulterant whatever. In Fluid Ju-Vis you get about double the usual quantity for the same money and we are satisfied that you will decide that it is at least equal in quality and flavour, to any Extract you have used. To make Gravies, Soups, Beef Teas at half the usual cost and like them better appeals to every one. A 2-oz. bottle of Ju-VisCan be bought of Grocers for aid. or send 61d. stamps for bottle to G. Foster Clark and Co., Maidstone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment.</head>
        <p>3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>The result of the musketry course in connection with the last annual training (1907) of this Battalion, has recently been notified to the Commanding Officer and reflects very creditably on all ranks.,. The officers have again won, for the second year in succession,the Challenge prize, a silver cup, awarded by the Militia Rifle Association for the best team of officers in the Western Command. The Battalion is the best shoot- ing battalion in the Westorn Command for 1907, with an average of 151 points against 139 made by the next bat, and has been awarded the " Starkie Challenge Cup," as the best shooting battalion of the Lancashire Militia Units for the year. " D " Company has won the "cross Challenge " shield as the best shooting company of the Lancashire Militia Units for the year, and also the Battalion " Inter-Company " challenge shield. The silver medal awarded by the Militia Rifle Association to the best individual shot, has been won by Sergeant W. Loin= For four years in succession the shoot;ng of the Battalion generally has been steadily improving. The recruits last year Improved 42 points per man on the previous year, and the Battalion figure of merit is 11 points higher. It is satisfactory to note that there are no third class shots, and that the men have taken such a very keen interest in the musketry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>When poaching eggs it is a good plan to put a teaspoonful of vinegar in the water; this sets the whites quickly, and keeps the eggs a nice shwe•</p>
        <p>To clean tarnished silver, moisten the whiting with methylated spirit or liquid ammonia iu- stead of with water. The cleaning will be done both quicker and better.</p>
      </div>
