        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 15, 1908.</p>
        <p>-IDHIWIIIATISM eared after ten years' suffering. I will send you the remedy for Is. with Instructions,ineluding a diet chart —J. WALL, 14, Robertson Terraoa. Hastings,</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER &amp; CO., " EXAMINE R" OFFICE, BEWSEY ST., WARRINGTON. IN VOICES RULED TOPATTE RN. PROSPECTUSES. PROGRAMMES.</p>
        <p>of the liquor traffic. It is vitally essential that every effort should be put forth to strengthen the hands of the Goverument in the prospective legislation to secure a measure of popular control over the licensed trade. Almost all the Liberal leaders are pledged to the principle of local option which, unfortunately, does not seem much nearer materializition than it did in 1891, when it was incorporated in the Newcastle programme. The reform can only be obtained step by step, but a big stride forward is expected rum the present Government. We hope War- rington people will make a real rally on Wednes- day night and hold high the flag of temperance which has drooped sadly too long.</p>
        <p>Proposals were made to the SIR HENRY AND House of Commons on Wed-</p>
        <p>THE PEERS, nesday in reference to the course of procedure on the two Scottish Bills,one dealing with small holdings, and the other with land valuation. They were described by Mr. BALFoun as "very unprincipled" This is almost on a level with the language used the other day by Lord ROBERT CECIL in connection with Mr. MCKENNA'S firm stand on the subject of training Colleges. But, after all, it means nothing. It is a fashibn of speech, transparent in its insincerity. The curiously disunited Opposition appears to have no policy of its own on any subject, and in this instance it is clearly without a case. It is possible —since, as Lord RANDOLPH CHURCHILL said, the business of an Opposition is to oppose—only to follow the time-honoured advice, and " abuse the plaintiff's attorney." Hard words may hurt, but, as we all know, they break no bones. It has been evident from the commencement of the Session that, in order to make up for the smallness of their numbers and the frailness of their argumenta- tive powers, gentlemen on the Conservative benches propose to employ such licence of 4 speech as will not stop short of licentiousness. In this respect Mr. BALFOUR is very little superior to,the most irresponsible of his supporters. He regards the resolution laid before the House of Commons on Wednesday by the PRIME MINISTER as the first positive stage in the cam- paign against the House of Lords, and we certainly cannot complain of this method of statement. What he says under that heed is quite true. A campaign must begin somewhere, and there must either be a combined movement of the Liberal forces against the House of Lords, or we must give u p all hope of giving legislative effect to the desires so clearly expressed by the people at the General Election.</p>
        <p>Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN makes no disguise of his intentions. If stronger measures are necessary than those which he now proposes to take, he is ready to produce them. He reminded the House of his previous declaration that, in the case of differences between the two Houses within the limits of a single Parliament, the decision of the House of Commons should prevail. And by " should "he means "must." But, before proceeding to any stern measures that ' may be ultimately required, he opens his campaign by the very moderate proposal that measures which have been tally approved by the House of Commons in one session of Parliament, and have then been thrown out of the House of Lords, shall only occupy a strictly limited number of days in another session, and shall then go to the Peers practically in the condition in which they were presented last year. Naturally, Mr. BALFOUR makes a great show of indignation. There is nothing else for him to do than to speak in the 'Ercles vein.</p>
        <p>The proposals themselves are as reasonable as any that were ever introduced to Parliament, and the reasons alduced in their behalf cannot be refuted ; they can only be denounced. The PRIME MINISTER himself boldly proclaims the novelty of his plan. What he proposes is to follow a course which has never before been taken, either by him- self or by any of his predecessors. But what dre the conditions which make such a course necessary ? Last year, the House of Commons ' spent a large part of the session over two Bills which were ultimately passed by large majorities. One of them passed its second reading by a majority of :239, and the other by a majority of 218. Absolutely no purposg is to be served by going over the same ground again. The Bills are aliaost unanimously endorsed by the Scottish people. On the other hand, according to Lord LaNsnowst, they were not rejected by the Upper Chamber on the ground of the demerits, but because the Lords had not had time to con- sider them. Tho PRVIE MINISTER'S plan will give the Peers abundance of time to consider them during the present Session of Parliament, and will thus put Lord LucsnowNn's sincerity to the test.</p>
        <p>What Warrington needs A TEMPERANCH more than anything else RILLY. to-day is a rousing rally to the cause of temperance. The town has long had the reputation of being drink-ridden, and we question whether it wag ever worse than it is to-day. At one time the temperance cause made consider able headway locally—and there ;t4 still a strong sentiment of the right sort in the town—but so far as active measures are concerned temperance in Warrington seems to be on the down grade. It is plain to all that the Tory party is bound hand and foot and purse, to the brewing interest,—and the circumstance is a distinct menace to the wel- fare of the community. The big meeting, under the auspices of the United Kmgdoin Alliance, to be held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening next should do much to stimulate the zeal of the temperance reformers and avowed opponents</p>
        <p>The compliment which the Tux Towx CouNcir. Town Council last week AND COUNCILLOR paid to Councillor Pember- PEMBERTON. ton in presenting him with a copy of the proceedings relating to the Limson Contract, Arbitration etc., seems to have acted like the red rag to the bull on the quarrelsome individual who occasionally airs his views in print under the thin disguise of "Ratepayer." We should think he is about the only " ratepayer " in Warrington with a mental vision so sadly distorted and obscured as was evidenced in his letter last Saturday. A fair sample of the class of argument our good friend 1" Ratepayer" does not hesitate to use is seen in the unmistakable insinuation that the " present " to Councillor Peniberton will cost the ratepayers—the other ratepayers, we suppose —a sum of £lOO. The writer of the letter waxes quite indignant at this "wanton and wicked waste "—probably with his tongue in his cheek— for he must know quite well that the present to Councillor Pemberton will not cost a penny, and that the Town Council never even considered the question of reprinting the proceedings at an expenditure of £lOO. But the letter was full of mis-statements of too significant a character to appear accidentaL Some of them reflect so seriously on the Non-Traders that Mr. Dodds has been constrained to depart from his usual custom never to notice anonymous letters, and in another column we publish a reply from him to " Ratepayer " which is so categorical and con- clusive that the work of Councillor Pemberton and the Non-Traders in regard to the Water Contract stands out clearly against a background of muddle, malice, mystery, and mismanagement.</p>
        <p>The ratepayer will not be THE COST OF pleased with the prospect EDUCATION. which is unfolded by the . estimates for the year 1908-9 which will be placed before the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening. He will not, however, be surprised that expenditure under this most important head continues on the up-grade for there has been so much leeway to make up educationally, that the penalty for past laggardliness is bound to be faced. Roughly speaking, the estimates will involve a rate of is. 7d.- as compared with is. 41d. last year, the deficiency to be made up from the rates being put down at -119,329 us. 3d. as against £16,671 1341. 4d. a year ago. The causes of the advance are not far to seek. New administrative work placed on the Committee by recent legislation—the medical ' inspection and feeding of school children—accounts for £BOO. We believe, however, that there is a consensus of opinion that if these new departments are carefully worked the gain to the community from the standpoint of health and stability will more than compensate for the outlay. The increased number of children which the authority is maintaining in industrial and deaf and dumb schools involves a considerable extra expenditure and another important item is a new payment of £2BO to the Lancashire County Council in respect of children from Warrington attending schools in the county area. Against this the contribution from the County Council to Warrington under similar circumstances is only £s2,and an important factor in the discrepancy is that whilst in Warring- ton the cost is only 12g. 6d. for each child, in Lancashire County the expenditure per head is 275. 6d. The expenditure on higher education shows a slightly decreased deiciency but on elementary education the deficiency advances by about £l,OOO. One of the chief causes of this is the opening of the new Belton School at Latch- ford and the consequent re-staffing of Christ Church School as a boys' school. The salaries of elementary school teachers absorb £29,317 14s. Bd. as compared with £28,341 Bs. The Finance Com- mittee requires £1,914 18s. 7d. for interest and instalments on loans for the new schools, as against £1,568 9s. id—the increase being due to the erection of the Bolton School. A saving of £6O is effected by the closing of Grappenhall-road School, so that roughly the cost of the Bolton School buildings to the ratepayers for the coming year will be £3OO. On the whole the normal working expenses of the authority are if at all diflerent from the preceding year, slightly less. The ratepayers will now be anxiously awaiting the Borough Budget, for upon the working of the other Corporation departments depends the great question of whether the increased demands for educational purposes will necessitate a rise in the general rates.</p>
        <p>-- Lord Esher has made so THE TRAINING many useful contributions OP THE to the solution a the ARMY OFFICER, military problem of the British Empire that any. thing he has to say upon the Army must be :istened to with respect. During recent years there has been a vast improvement in the training of the officers of the Army. At the time of the 1 Boer War the British officer was decried as a I person who had not given himself ta the study of his profession. In some cases the allegation was true, but in many others it was most unjust. At the present time serious work is unquestionably the order of the day among the younger as well as the older officers. What is lacking now is not the desire, the serious intention to seek efficiency, but the inducement to stick at the work. Lord Esher points out that Mr. Haldane has already done something to encourage young officers to specialise. That is his solution of the difficulty. He would like to see the Staff College reformed so that it would provide an inducement to young men to work at military history, military geography, and at strategy and tactics. All this is really obvious. After all, the only legitimate subjects of study for serious members of the military profes- sion are those which have been named. What is wanted is the provision of more complete facilities for the acquirement of proficiency in them, and the encouragement, not of spasmodic work, but of continuous application to them. Let the War Office adopt Lord Esher's idea of extending the inducements for young officers and of instituting annual tests of efficiency, which shall be some sort of guide in the selection for posts, and the training system of the British officer will have made very considerable progress.</p>
      </div>
