      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Village Blacksmith.„ Next week at the Court Theatre is announced the welcome return visit of Messrs. Dottridge, Longden, I and Livesey's " Village Blacksmith " Company. We have that clever actor Mr. Sam Livesey, in the title role, who will assuredly look the part as he is a man of brawny muscle and fine presence, and will be favourably remembered as " Ronjarre " in "The King of Crime." We are informed that this piece has had phenomenal success at all theatres visited during the tour, and has in abundance everything to command pros- perity—a good company, excellent scenery, and a plot that rivets the attention during the whole of the five interesting and sensational acts. The wrestling match between Mr. Livesey and the" villain" is the talk of every town visited. Full houses are therefore expected at the Court Theatre next week. This company is under the same management as the "Aladdin " Pantomime which visited the Royal Court Theatre just recently.</p>
        <p>CONTINUOUS-NE-OLE-CT—OF THE EYES is the most fruitful source of serious eye trouble. A dainty pair of Spectacles or Eyeglasses fitted by Johnston Watson will preserve your eyes and give lasting comfort. JOHNSTON WATSON, EYESIGHT SPECIALIST, 24, Bridge-street, Warrington. Low Charges, Satisfaction Guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>ri="7:l7.</p>
        <p>*7:IT4 The "Evening News" recording the demise of "The Tribune" says "Another London Journal Strikes Its Flag." Surely more important than striking the flag is the blow to the stall.—" London Opinion." The lately revealed practice of supplying beer on credit in Warrington, reminds us of an old "Punch" picture of a fire at a country inn. One of the most regular clients is standing by and exhorting the fire- men to turn the hose" on the slate." ** * •</p>
        <p>There is a strong Nationalist group in our Local Parliament and the House is occasionally treated to the pure Hibernian brogue. On Wednesday evening the hon. member for East Mayo rose to move the adjournment of the debate and was at once asked by the Speaker what constituency he represented. " Aist Moo" was the reply which puzzled many members, and even the Speaker had to ask for a repetition of the answer before it dawned upon him that East Mayo was the place referred to.</p>
        <p>The 13ess-o'th'-Barn Band have been in every district of importance in England,have been heard in Scotland, Ireland, and Wales, and have played before white and black population. 4 of foreign lands, but they have yet to appear in the Isle of Man. It is strange but true that the natives of the Fiji Islands, in the Pacific, heard this famous organisation before the Mans. It is very probable, however, that before the coining summer is over the bandsmen will have paid their initial visit to the " Little Man Island." * * *</p>
        <p>"We wish," wrote the editor of the " Tartown Trumpet," "to correct an error which crept into our issue of last week. In describing the unfortunate runaway accident on Main-street we wrote : While waiting for the arrival of the ambulance, Doctor Skinner, who was fortunately present, took the victim's pulse.' It was the printer who carelessly changed the' l' in the last word to 'r.' We make this correction in justice to Doctor Skinner, whose fees are always moderate, and who never presents a bill in I advance. Office over Jed Kimballs' Drug Store."— " Cleveland Leader." * *</p>
        <p>According to the Chief Constable there is a method. of obtaining beer on credit in Warrington, and it has been taken advantage of by workmen to the extent of nine or ten pints of beer between 6 p.m. and 11 p.m. One man has credit and " makes a book "in which the drink supplied to each workman is entered, the owner of the book settling up at the end of each week, and making a nice commission out of the thirsty workers who have to pay him at the rate of 3d. per pint. This is an ingenious method of demoralising the wage-earner, which the Chief Constable and all right thinking people condemn. We hope the magis- trates will mark their sense of disapprobation at the adjourned Sessions. ,</p>
        <p>Two cases of the fatal fascination exercised by strong drink came before the Liverpool city coroner on Wednesday. One was that of a dock labourer, forty-one years of age, who was shown to have habitually drunk up his wages at each week-end, though the alcohol usually produced a fit. In the second case, a widow woman, seventy-six years of age was shown to have earned ss. weekly, while the parish allowed her 3s. 6d. and a former employer 2s. 6d. Out of the lls, she paid 2s. for lodging and fire, while the bulk of the balance of Os. went in intoxicants until the inevitable end came. She seemed scarcely to have been a proper subject for outdoor 'relief. _ * *</p>
        <p>• There will be universal regret among those in public life in Lancashire at the retirement of the Right Hon. Sir John T. Hibbert from the chairmanship of the Lancashire County Council after 19 years' service in that position. " Impaired health and fail- ing eyesight " are the pathetic reasons ascribed by one who has dignified a great office and to whom is due much more largely than he himself admits, "the building up of an administrative system worthy of Lancashire, which has gained for us a high, if not the foremost,position in the management of county affairs." Sir John Hibbert is 84, and is a wonderful example of abounding activity continued to extreme old age. He first sat for Oldham as long ago as 1862, and, with breaks, represented that borough until 1803. He has five times held office in Liberal Administrations, but his 19 years at the head of county affairs at Preston will be his chief title to fame. His residence is still at Grange-over-Sands, but he writers his letter of resignation from Torquay.* " Life's Almanac." The subject seemed a simple one and those who attended the Rev. G. flarbottle's lecture at Bewsey-road Church on Monday evening were curious to know how he would trent it. Of course, they knew he would have no difficulty in making it interesting, and they were not disappointed for the lecture was one of the most entertaining which Mr. flarbottle has yet delivered in Warrington. He adopted the plan of selecting leading dates in his own life and conjuring up visions of what was happening in the world at those particular times. The lecture thus touched upon such widely diverse topics as the death of Wordsworth and Tennyson's accession to the laureateship, the Crimean War and the Indian Mutiny, the promulgation of evolution by Darwin, the Civil War in America and the war of liberation in Italy, the work of Thackeray, and the Wesleyan Conference of 1873. Mr. Harbottle occupies a warm place in the hearts of Warrington Wesleyans, who look back with pleasure on his ministry in this circuit, and they were delighted to see him looking as well as ever. * * * Mr. Lloyd George's promise of an inquiry into the whole question of railways, especially in their rela- tions with the State, has been received with general satisfaction. There are practically only two methods of conducting such an injury—either by a Parlia- mentary Committee or by a Royal Commission. The former mode of inquiry would be rather slow, for a committee can only sit while Parliament is in session, and many of the men most competent to conduct such an investigation might be excluded. It is expected, therefore, that tile inquiry will be carried out by a Royal Commission of a very able and representative character. The inquiry at the best must be a pro- longed one, extending over probably two or three years, and no one doubts that it will lead to the pro- posal of very important changes in the position of British railways, especially in their relation to the State. One of, the duties of the Commission will be to investigate the working of railways owned by the State in Germany, Belgium, and other countries. The reports of officers sent by the Board of Trade to inquire into the operation of the State railways in Germany are highly favourable.</p>
      </div>
