      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Wall. Many of our readers will regret to learn that the Rev. Harry Wall (assistant minister to Wycliffe Church, and minister at Cockhedge Mission), is TRAVELLING will:lour A TICKET. seriously ill with an attack of rheumatic fever follow- At Widnes, on Thursday morning Thomas Charnock, ing upon influenza, a well-known farmer, of Ditton, was charged with Junior Liberal Association, travelling on the 4th ult. from Lime-street to Ditton Junction without paying his fare.-51r. Grounds, the The weekly meeting of this Association was held at night inspector, stated that he saw the defendant leave Gregory's Cafe on Monday evening, Councillor the 5.35 train and walk across the metals to get out on Hawthorn presiding. It was an " impromptu even- the highway. Witness followed him and asked for his ing " and a variety of topics were discussed. ticket, but he replied that he had neither ticket n•ir For Perfection in BACON try ASHLEY'S.—Advt. money. Later he offered witness is. to say nothing about it.—The Bench imposed a fine of R 1 and costs. Warrington Bird Fancier's Success. SUDDEN DEATH OF A RUNCORN PUBLICAN. Mr. A.Stout,of Warrington was awarded a prize at the The death took place as the result of an epileptic tit annual cage bird show of the London and Provincial on Sunday morning of Mr. Charles William Dawson Ornithological Society, held at the Crystal Palace in licensee of the Holyhead Harbour fun, High-street the class for Yorkshire canaries, buff, clear, or ticked Runcorn. The deceased, who was 5S years of age, was in the novice section. at business as usual throughout Saturday. He had held ' LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and the licence only three months. He had previously been Bronchial troubles. 9,1 d. &amp;1/li. Of Chemists.—Advt licensee of the Fariaworth Arms, Fara worth. The fi -qral took place at Runcorn Cemetery on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>• ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>• ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>--- BUTTER FRAUD AT Sr. HELENS. Thomas Crosby, alias Hartley, of Wakefield, was charged, at St. Helens, with obtaining money by false pretenoes.—The evidenoe was that ten days ago Sergeant Shaw saw the man in a shop in Dake-street °tiering something for sale. lie was behaving in a disgracefu manner. The Ulcer interfered, and' found that he hap some butter in a basket, which he was selling without having a market permit. He had sold some of the stuff at Is. 2d. per pound to three persons. The police proved . .</p>
