        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME</p>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR. Edited by John Workman. Z.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, "Examiner " Office, Warrington awl should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week. tteaderg of this column -are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and erldeavouf to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-dx-pluni.e may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the columns.</p>
        <p>1 I have received the followin., letter from one of my 1 o occasional correspondents :- 1 "Der John u,Torkman, —Oh! yau blood-thirsty villain! See what Counciitor J. Fuulton-Smith said of you last 'Thursday evening (6th just) before the Junior Oonzervatives.</p>
        <p>Alluding to the outrage at Lisbon, the speaker said he feared this country would go to that extreme if they did not set their faces against Socialism, which was nothing more nor less, and could not stop short of anarchy. (Hear, hear.) Whatever happened they must have law, order and discipline, and always have a recognised authority, otherwise there could only be confusion amongst the people. (Hear, hear.) It was for them as Unionists to believe in the power of the monarchy, and to fight against the insidious doctrines which were put forward. There was another deep laid scheme, and that was to upset the religion of our country. If their religion was threatened then . .</p>
        <p>their lib-antes arid enjoinents might, as welt go to the winds. (Applause.) There is some intelligence abroad, is.ft there ?" Yours, Y.Z. •</p>
        <p>I understand that Councillor J. Fulton-Smith-is the Chairman of the Education authority, and I presume that that office requires a gentleman of some education to fill it with dignity and efficiency. I also imagine that an educated man keeps himself well posted up with current political and social signs of the times or events. If lam correct, then in all sincerity I would ask him when he goes .to church on the morrow, to take particular notice of the Ninth Commandment. * * *</p>
        <p>And I would suggest'. that the " brainy " individuals who congregate at the Junior Conservative Club should not be satistied with the " puff-paste " supplied from Sankey-street seniors, and might also, tor a short time, give up their "beer and skittles" and do a little thinking for themselves. It must become monotonous to them to hear the 83313 political piffis S 3 often propounded by the heads, of local Conservatism, and perhaps an interlude in the form of a speech by a political opponent might produce a pleasing and edifying effect. Ono never mows!</p>
        <p>S3cialists are not only-accused of being " bbod- thirsty," but are,, by some, looked upon as fanatics advocating an earthly Paradise. They are asking for the Kingdom of Gad to be made possible on earth - ' they must surely not have read their Bible aright (?) Mr. lan Malcolm, the prospective Cinservative candi- date for North Salford in speaking there the other evening is reported to have said : 'Socialism, however good it might be in a com- pany of angels looked alter by arch-angels was a very don .trul speculation in a community Of human beings. Under Sonialisin they were all to work under One master, the State ; their duties were to be assigned and their wages paid by the State? It I could only be a collection of human beings like I themselves, who would be paid high salaries. There inaquality came in at (Nine. %Va.) was going to elect the State Government ?" Mien will politicians learn politics ? Our old friend, Sir John Brunner, M.P., addressing a Liberal demonstration m Middlewich, on 'Thursday evening, declared that the Small Holdings Act would have far-reaching and extremely beneficial conse- quences, aud the younger generation would find that 1008 begun with a new chapter of history tor the Irural population.</p>
        <p>.. - Dealing with the Socialist crusade, Sir John admitted that there was a great legitimate field for socialising the national delence,the Post oJice,</p>
        <p>roads, lighting, and even railways, but a Socialist state, to be managed by officials, meant the nega- w tioa, denial, and abolition of freedom. Further, the intelligent effort nosy naturally directed to individual benefit would disappear; men would work less, and instead of the people's prosperity being ine,reased it would be deareased. True Liberalism meant true progress arid the salvation of the helpless and suffering. Perhaps Sir John will tell us why State offizials should not be trusted to manage a State as well as a railway ?</p>
        <p>JOHN.WORKUAN. MUNICIPAL BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>My word John, Warrington is at last moving "municipally "and no mistake, but at the same time I suggest that the majority of our City Fathers have been unwise in supporting Councillor Pemberton's resolutions re new bowling greens in Victoria Park, Orford-lane, St. Elphin's Park and Stnkey Recreation Grounds " all to be completed in time to be played upon during the coming summer months." The only serious objection that can be urged against these Oiluertiau proposals is that it will not be the Town Council representatives themselves, but the ratepayers of Warrington, who will be in reality financially responsible for the preparation and upkeep of those prospective recreative centres. It is a generally admitted fact in bowling circles that the municipal green in Victoria Park, Latchford, has been very seriously impaired through the undue haste'with which it was thrown opan to toe public. Even were it possible to at once commence preparing these four proposed greens, it is highly improbable that they ; could advantageously be utilised for bowling purposes during the coming summer season. Further than this 1 I agree with Alderman Bolton that it is farcical to pass these resolutions because the forinaiities could not be concluded in time.</p>
        <p>"REASON?' THE LABOUR PARTY AND SODIA.LISAI.</p>
        <p>! In accordance with an intimation p last week's 1" Voice " I will deal this week with the Lab3ur Party Conference and Socialism. The amendment to the constitution of the Party was submitted by the Paper Stainer's Union, which, if carried, would have compelled all the trades unions affiliated to adopt Socialism as their ultimate object, or otherwise to withdraw from the Party. After a lengthy discussion, the amendment was defeated by 951,000 to 01,000. IThis decision was said to be, by the daily Press, a Ihuge defeat for Socialism, for on the contents bills of the daily newspapers we saw placarded in large type such phrases as" Socialists Routed" ; " Great Blow to Socialism " ; "Socialists Beateu by Ten t ) One," etc. Thu-. may have been instrumental in effecting a sale of the various newspapers, but it was certainly very foolish and misleading. An attempt was made to amend Clause 3 of the constitution by inserting the words " or Slcialist " after the word Labour in the following paragraph :—" Candidates must appear before their constituencies as Labour Candidates only." This amendment was defeated by 016,000,t0 82,0.00. Yet another attempt was made t ) interfere with what has been so far a very satisfactory policy. A resolu- tion was submitted by the London Trades Council asking the Conference "to instruct the Executive to formulate a programme based upon the resolution passed at the Annual Conference, to which all candi- dates must be pledged." This was really an indirect method of trying to compel all candidates of the Labour Party to embrace Socialism as part of their programme. The resolution was defeated by a huge majority. On time last day of the Conference a good deal of interest was centred upon the resolution on Socialism which appeared on the agenda in the name of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers. The resblution asked the Conference to say " that in their opinion the time has arrived when the Labour Party should have, as -a definite object the socialisation of the means of life &amp;c." A mud sensation was caused by the Standing Orders Committee recommending that, in view of the previous day's decision, the resolution should be withdrawn. Tue reeetrunendation, however, was defeated, and the resolution was therefore sub- mitted to the Conference. After a brief discussion, during which lir. David Shackleton' M. P., vigorously opposed the resolution, it was carried by 574,000 to 469,600.</p>
        <p>These figures were not fully representative of the delegates' opinion, for many Socialists voted against the resolution. The voting took place amidst sup- pressed excitement, bat when the figures were announced there was a most enthusiastic demonstra- tion by the Socialists, a few of them making an attempt to sing the " Red Flag." It was some minutes before the Conference was able to proceed with the other busine,s. Some of the delegates shouted, " look out for the contents bills to-morrow " referring to the contents bills of the daily newspapers which each morning had been a source of amusement to the delegates. In commenting on the vote on the Engineers' resolution one of the widely read morning papers referred to it as a tragical development, and proceeded to chastise the delegates. It said that "the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>majority of the delegates have found it impossible to maintain their self-restraint for more than twenty-four hours, so anxious were they to display their power, and like most revolutionaries they have the faults of excitable children. So far from 'drawing other groups of working classes representatives into the party, as was hoped for, this outburst of fanaticism must offer a serious menace to its present dimensions." When we read this, and other similar criticisms we smiled. Time will prove whether these gloomy predictions will come true. 1 find that it is impossible in this issue to deal with the criticisms which have been published.</p>
        <p>Next week I hope to be able to do so and give my opinion on the position and action of the Labour Party.</p>
        <p>A CONFERENCE DELEGATE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>HOUSING REFORM.</head>
        <p>HOUSING REFORM.</p>
        <p>Lecture on the Co-Partnership Movement.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON SCHEME.</p>
        <p>A large audience assembled in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Thureday eveaing to heir a lecture by Mr. Frederica Litchfield, F.S.S., on " Ca-partner- ship in Housing." The lecture was one of a series pro- moted by the Education Committee of the Co-operative Society. Mr. C. E. Parser presided and amongst those present were :—Aldermau Bennett, Couacillor Nield, , Messrs. F. R. Roberts, G. %VA,lker, W. 11. Skerburn, W. Mason and members of the committee. The GlialitalaN said Mr. Litchfield was certainly a theorist in the matter of housing and he was associated with a very successful movement which was spreading very rapidly in different parts of the country. Mr. LarcliFIELD proceeded with his lecture which proved most interesting, being illustrated with a series I of beautiful views. tie said he believed all co-operators II would agree that every man and woman ought to live in a suitable house with a garden attached, and where the conditions were such as to make it easier to be happy than miserahle. He had no hesitation in saying that if he had to live in the houses where some of our people lived he should ue miseraele,aud instead of being a total abstainer he would in all probability visit the public- house where he might get some chauce of enjoyment. • Pictures of some of the dingy courts in our great cities were then shown and the lecturer said it ought to be made impossible for such dwellings to continue. It was a difficult task bet he believed if co-operators put their shoulders to the wheel they would very I quiekly attain a far better state of affairs. As I a contrast to the conditioos mentioned, the lecturer I then called attention to a lovely view of Chatsworth I House. Mutt they wanted, he said, was something , between the two extremes. Ouring the past fifty years, 23 Acts of Parliament had been passed dealing with the housing question and to-day it was still oue of the most pressing political matters. If women had had a hand in the matter, it would have been vastly different and he lived the day would come When the women would have a far greater voice in the erection of houses and in the planning of towns than they had to-day. (Applause.) Mr. Litchfield exhibited some striking diagrams showing how crowded conditions affected the death rate. Continuing he pointed out that 13 million people in England and Wales lived in 2,600,000 tene- ments of four rooms and less, and these tenements were placed on 43,000 acres. There were 12 millions of people living on 152,000 acres and there were 7.; millions living on nearly 20,000,000 acres. This showed that the land was not properly divided. There VMS not the co-partnership aud co-operation which was necessary, and they themselves were a great deal to blame for it. Mr. Litchfield- then reviewed the successful garden city movements inaugurated by Mr. W. H. Lever, M.P., and Mr. Cadbury for the benefit of their workpeople at Port Sunlight and Bouraville, and some very pleasing pictures were shown. Mr. Lever, he said, had demonstrated th tt at 7 years of age Port Sunlight children were 3 inches taller and 71bs. heavier than the childreu of the same age in Liverpool, and that tre• II years of age they were 5',1 inches taller and 20Ibs. heavier. Mr. Cadeury had ale) shown that the average boy living in houru villa VMS considerably bigget , than the working-class boy in Birmingham. Mr. Litchfield I then dealt with the Tenants' Co-Parteership movement founded by Mr. Henry Vivian, M.P., which enabled the people to combiue to erect comfortable houses for themselves. There were now seventeen of these societies with property amounting to over a quarter of a million and in the next three or four years they would crest two millions worth of homes for the people. (Applause.) Last WOOS a society was registered in IVarringtoaaud the shares would be £1 each. The dividend on shares would be limited to 5 per cent. and no 0110 Could hold more than £2OO. Tnere was a federation of the various societies, so that if any person wanted to seli his shares the Society would purchase them at par, charging at first 5 par cent, com- mission which would in time be reduced to 2 per cent. A gentleman had told him that night that if the workers of Warrington would promise to invest £seo he would put 'in another £530, so that they could start with £!,000. Mr. Litchfield then alluded to the Garden City Corn- pay at Letchworth which purchased land and let it to the Tenants' Society, and said there were 220 cottages there worth .€70,000. Me rates were only 2s. in the .4 ' and as the profits would go te public purposes they , hoped that in time there would be no rates at all. In the gardea subarbs the tenants always had to pay their own rates and taxes. They believed every man should be a citzen and take an interest in local government. Mr. Litchfield gave details of the schemes at Sevenoaks, Hampstead and Birmingham and coaeluded by warmly commending the movement in Warrington, remarking that the Plans accepted by the Garden City Company were delightful. (Applause.) Questions were invited and Mr. .W. BiBBV asked how</p>
        <p>they proposed to raise the in-aney in ‘'rer-ringt;o-n..71 Mr. LiTCHFIELD said there were people in Warring- ton and district with money. They wanted a safe investment and he was there to sty that if they put money iaba the Warrington Tenants' Saciety it would be four-fold blessed. It would be a safe investment, yielding interest,and the money would bless theeworkers who would receive from 10s. to 12s. of every invested in the Society. The investors would have the joy of knowing that they were helping the workers to ' provide happy homes and the nation would be blessed becaose people viva id be healthier and live lonz,er. fie was certain there were people in Warrington and 1 district who would he prepared to invest in i per cent. loan stock by taking up a few shares. The tenants would also be able to invest and would receive 5 per cent, lie thought it was the best security for,eapital that oould he found. Everytuing would depend on the way ia which the Society " catignt on " but there was no reason why dividend should net be paid in three years. , Mr. TROW contended that the members of the Co-operative Soeiety had already a mdte.ne which was 20_t1mes better than this new movement.</p>
        <p>The question of the price of land was also raised. Tile CHAIRMAN said the Co-oper ttive Society was. not committed to the scheme and the question must be an individual one to a grect extent. If me nbers of the Co-operative Society had a better thing he would urge them to go on with it and Wish them " God speed." The parent company in Warriugton had paid a little over .£2OO per acre for their land, but in addition they would have to reckon the cost of roadinaking. If the matter was in right hands, they had the conditions in Warrington to make it a sitcom He deprecated the I spirit of anybody who would oppose other people join- ing the movement bacause it diettot commend itself to - them. Mr. iirsnitar moved a vote of thanks to the Lecturer and Chairman and said ha felt coall lent the movement in Warrington Was ill the right bands ad that it would be snacessfnl.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. FAARNR in seconding said he was heart and soul in the movement, and he felt sore it would be enthusiastically taken up. The vote was heartily acoorded. During the evening selections were giveti in adruir- ablestyle lo`y the Co-operative Aisle Voice Choir tinder the conductorship of Mr. A. E. 'Prow and qr. C. Furness delighted the andienco with his recitations. Letters regretting inahilitv to attend had beim received from the Mayor, Sir tlilbert Graeaell, :sir. W. P. Rylands, Jr.N L. Greening, sir. H. antuerts, Rev. Canon Stevenson, Councillor Fulton-Smith, ntr. W. Monks, Mr. E. J. Hall, Nfr. W. LI. StiOSil3ltl, Or. Aarkel and Or. Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Holy Trinity Cricket Club.</head>
        <p>Holy Trinity Cricket Club.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Thursday night at the Holy Unity Club in connection with the formation of a cricket club for the coming year to represent Holy , Trinity. The meeting was well attended. The follow- ing were elected for the coining year: President. Rev. E. C. E Carleton; vice-presidents, Mr. Gill, Mr. James Harding,•sen , Mr. Joseph Hesketh (subject to their approval) ; secretary, A. lidbroak : treasurer, H. S iiorrabin ; captain, Harold Harding ; sub- captain, J. D. Smith; committee, Messrs. P. Barnes, T. Worthington, A. Holbrook, T. Jefferson, and H. S. Hoirabin. It was agreed that the club should enter the Warrington and District Junior Cricket League for the coming year. The late secretary and treasurer (H. S. Horrabin) in reading his report &amp;entioued that he was glad to say that they had had a fairly successful year last year but he hoped that they -would have greater success this year than last.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs ata ,Colds ; never fails. 2/9.—Advt.</p>
