        <p>Marriage at Flixton. An interesting wedding took place at the Flixton Parish Church on Tuesday, the Rev. A. W. Smith, M.A. (the rector) officiating, in the presence of a fashionable congregation. The contracting parties were Mr. Robert Montague Irwell, only son of Mrs. Irwell, of Ac.:urb-street, Whitwo- th Park, and Angelina (" Lena ") eldest daughter of Mr. A. A. Cowley, advertising contractor, of Manchester and tirmston. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a French grey pinafore robe, trimmed with lace and embroidery, with a white silk hat and ostrich feather, her ornaments being a snake pendant with sapphire adornments, and ruby and diamond bracelet, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaid was Miss G. L. Cowley (sister), who wore a biscuit-coloured dress trimmed with brown velvet and lace. She also wore a pearl and amethyst pendant, the gift of the bridegroom. The best man was Mr. R. G. Cowley, eldest brother of the bride. At the close of the ceremony the party drove to Manchester, where a well-attended reception was held at the Midland Hotel. The presents were numerous and beautiful. Subsequently Mr. and Mrs. Irwell departed for the South of England, and on their return they will take up their residence at " Rivington," Island- road, nix ton.</p>
        <p>For 200 Years the Secret of Grannie Ointment has een kept in one Family. Its curative action is wonderful in all cases of WOUNDS. CUTS, BRUISES, BAD LEGS, and all SKIN TROUBLES. It at once soothes and heals, and cannot hurt the most delicate skin. In many cases of very long standing it has quickly cured, even when doctors have despaired. Fran for lotamps. GRANNIE COMPANY, NORM/IC II</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. MATTITEIT., (Nferthyr Tyclvil, who sat with the Socialist group) said that while he feared the measure vvbich had been introduced was but another attempt at tinkering with a great problem rather than n serious, down-right attempt to solve it—(bear, hear) —he did not say that taking the clauses and looking at them point by point, they did not contain something that was exceedingly good. Looking at them in the light of temperance reform, however, they fell very far below what was absolutely necessary in the</p>
