      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>TUBERCULOSIS AND WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Twenty years' observation of phthisis or a district in Dartmoor and North Devon, has convinced investigators that populations exposed to strong prevalent rainy winds have a higher death-rate from consumption than populations sheltered from them. The contrasts in the phthisis death-rate in the sheltered and exposed parishes are very marked.</p>
        <p>IMMUNITY OF HIBERNATING ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>Some experiments carried out ty MM. Blanchard mid Blatin, and of which they give an account in the Bulletin of the Zoo- logical Society of France,' indicate that hiber- nating animals may be immune to diseases caused by micro-organisms. They inocu- lated active and hibernating marmots with various microbes. In some eases the active animals were susceptible, but when hiber- nating they were absolutely immune. The parasites seemed to perish on account of the low temperature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WHAT ARE OAK APPLES?</head>
        <p>WHAT ARE OAK APPLES?</p>
        <p>The galls of which the oak-apple is the most familiar example, occur on v. great number of different plants. Of recent years they have been found on those lowly vege- tables known as lichens. Professor W. Zopf found some of these, in the form of deformed and swollen branches, on a species of lichen from the island of Kullen. Examination showed they were caused by mites. Another lichen, which had been classed as a distinct variety, was found to owe its difference merely to the presence of galls. Our authority believes that another so-called variety will, on examination, be found to be the species deformed by gall growths.</p>
        <p>SEAWEED THAT CARRIES ()FT OYSTERS. An insidious enemy has invaded the oyster beds of the French coasts. The Minute spores of a seaweed came floating up the Channel on the Gulf Stream, and found resting-places on the oyster beds of Morbihan. Here it was first detected. Since then it has spread to Quiberon, Cherbourg, Gatteville, and St. Vaast. The seaweed grows on the shell of the oyster, and is of an oval shape, solid at first, and afterwards filled with water. They may reach the size of a hen's egg, or even of a fist. Left uncovered by the tide Lney split, and loose their water. This is replaced by air, which is imprisoned by the next rise of the tide. The seaweed now acts as a balloon, raises the oyster from the bottom, and floats with it out to sea. According tb M. Fabre-Domergue, no fewer than 400,000 oysters have thus been carried away from the oyster-beds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ANCESTRAL INSTINCT.</head>
        <p>ANCESTRAL INSTINCT.</p>
        <p>That strange perversion of habit which sometimes leads a well-trained sheep dog to become a destroyer of the flock is well known. Not long ago at Strathearn in Scot- land, a number of them associated themselves into a small pack, hunting the sheep after the manner of wolves, going from one farm to another in the district, and doing a con- siderable amount of damage. It would ap- pear, the Globe thinks, to be a reversion to ancestral instinct repressed by many genera- tions of training. For there is a considerable amount of evidence that modern breeds of dogs are the result of the ancient domestica- tion of probably several species of wolf and fox. Different nations, that is to say, have domesticated the native species of one of these animals, and these have been afterwards mingled by crossing. The sheep dog has pro- bably more of the wolf in it than other breeds. The bones that are found along with the im- plements of the. Stone Age ehew that primi- tive man had domesticated both the wolf and the fox. And these bones pass by gradual transition to those of undoubted dogs. In many cases it is difficult to decide whether a given bone should be assigned to a wolf or a dog, on the one hand, or a fox or a dog on the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SPOTTED LIONS AND PUMAS.</head>
        <p>SPOTTED LIONS AND PUMAS.</p>
        <p>Lions from East Africa retain more or less distinct traces of the spots of their childhood even when full grown; and it is quite evident that both lions and pumas are descended from fully-spotted ancestors. By an examina- tion of the form and arrangement of the spotting in the cubs of the two species, Mr. R. I. Pocock has been endeavouring to ascer- tain the nature of the markings of their re- spective forebears. In the case of lion cubs, the author shews that the pattern of the markings is intermediate in character be- tween the stripes of the tiger and the rosette- like spots of the leopard, although inclining perhaps more to the former type. Another distinctly tiger-like feature in the lion cub is the presence of a white patch over the eye, which disappears in the adult. As regards the stripes of the tiger, these certainly seem to be derived from the rosettes of the leopard; the double striping frequently seen being a remnant of the rosettes. If these con- clusions are accurate, comments Kvowledge, it is clear that lions, tigers, and leopards (and, it may be added, jaguars) form a closely-allied group. Puma cubs, on the other hand, present a pattern quite unlike that seen in any of the foregoing, and thus indi- cate the marked distinctness of Felix con- color from the lion and tiger group. Pumas may accordingly be regarded as large, uni- formly-coloured derivatives of one of the groups of smaller, and probably American, cats.</p>
        <p>SIGNAL LIGHTS FOR BREECHES BUOYS. After having been in use for over a cen- tury without improvement or change, the breeches buoy used by organised life-saving crews has been improved by a device that has been adopted by the United States life-saving service. The main difficulty with the breeches buoy as heretofore constructed, says the Scientific American, has been that at night, when it left the shore, no one knew definitely where it was, whether it had reached the wrecked ship or not, or whether anyone had gotten in, unless the mariners had lights on the vessel. John W. Dalton, of Gloucester, known to --. I mariners all along the Massachusetts coast, is A RAY OF SUNSHINE. ! the inventor of the devices which are known IT COMES LIKE A RAY OF SUNSHINE . as " Dalton's auto-signal safety breeches THIS GOOD NEWS FROM EARLESTOWN. buoy." The improvement in question com- prises, speaking in general terms, a small GOOD NEWS TO MANY HERE. case mounted on an inflated rubber cushion -Mrs. S. J. Williams. 225, Warg,rave-road, Earlestown, and surrounded by four small hollow posts says:—" For two and a half years I suffered with and on top to a square steel spreader. In the kidney complaint which followed on rheumatic fever. The backache was chronic, only sometimes worse than case is a storage battery that operates a set of lamps. One light, a green one, shews to- of the back. The headaches and giddiness were others ; they were shooting pains which troubled me j when I did anything, and were right across the small 1 wards the shipwreck when the device is terrible and my eyesight was so bad that I had to wear STARTED OUT TO THE VESSEL ; glasses. 1 had nasty hot flushes to my face, and always felt tired, very nervous and easily depressed; my breath the other light, a white one, shows down was very short and I was soon exhausted. The heart ! through the rubber cushion into the breeches, was very troublesome, getting alarmingly bad when 1 enabling the shipwrecked people to