      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Warrington v. Bradford.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Bradford.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men, through illness and injuries,•had to take almost a reserve team to Bradford, and no one was surpi ised at them losing. The team was weak enough as chosen, but when Taylor and Isherwood cried off there were only two of the regular backs left. For half the game Warrington made a plucky struggle, but ail injury to Fish further handicapped them later, and the home club won comfortably. Fine weather favoured the fixture, and there was a gate of about 2,000. Teams : Bradford Northern—Gunn, back; Neil, Scliolefield, Mosby and O'Rourke, three-quarter backs; Burman and Roberts, half-backs; Mann, Rees, Walton, Haley, Feather and Francis, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back; Fish, Foster, Jordan and Watkin, three-quarter backs; Daintith and-Lloyd, half backs • Jenkins, Jolley, Belton, Harmer, Shugara and P. Boardoan, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. Mainwaring,Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Jackson, Hunslet, and H. Sewell, Leeds. Uneventful play near the centre was witnessed during the initial stages, Warrington gradually taking up the attack, and for some time Bradford were kept busily defending. Surman eventually relieved to the centre with a tricky run. Bradford had the misfortune to be penalised on their own quarter-line, but Fish, who took the kick for Warrington. failed at goal from a fairly easy position. Bradford were still unable to make much headway, and being again penalised, Daintith landed a fine goal for the visitors. A strong rush by Bradford then took play up the field, but off-side tactics robbed them of what looked like a certain score. War- rington were now defending, an both Scholelleld and Gunn had drop kicks at the Warrington goal, the ball each time falling short. Fish then relieved with a good run, but was hurt in. colliding with Gunn and had to leave the field for a time. Gunn equalad for Bradford but Jordan again gave Warrington the lead with an excellently judged drop goal. Half-time score : Warrington 2 goals (4 points), Bradford Northern, I goal (2 points). On resuming Bradford at once took the upper hand, and the footwork of the forwards improving, the home backs got possession repeatedly, some nice pass- ing movements being executed. Surman soon made matters level with an easy penalty goal, and then Rees ran in with a good try, from which Burman kicked a goal. The Warrington line was repeatedly assailed, Neil, Scholefield, Mosby, and O'Rourke, time and again almost breaking through. Eventually the. ball went nut to Nell, and the speedy left-winger raced over for a fine try. Scholefield took the goal-kick, but the distance beat him. Result :—Bradford Northern 3 goals and 2 tries (12 points), Warrington 2 goals (4 points). NbW Zealand Win the Chelsea Test Match.</p>
        <p>The second test match between the New Zealanders and a team representing the Northern Union was played at Chelsea on Saturday, providing Londoners with their first opportunity of seeing how Rugby football is played by professionals. The game was watched by nearly 15,000 people, which was considered a most satisfactory attendance, seeing,that there was a greet counter-attraction in the shape of an international match at Richmond. No doubt many had come to see the New Zealanders as mulch as an exposition of the Northern Union game. It so happened that they saw them at their best, for the New Zealanders played a tine game—their best, in' fact, since they landed. Wynward and Wrigley behind a winning serum had a great time, and they initiated many clever passing movements. But the most brilliant men of all were Smith, Messenger and Turtill, the last-named even surpassing the fine display he gave in the first test game at Leeds. Smith's try was the outstanding feature of the game, and roused the spectators to great enthusiasm. He got the ball from Wynward well in his own half, dodged several opponents. and then ran into what seemed a ruck of Northern Union backs. But he handed oft first one and then another, and eventually wriggling himself free of Taylor the full back scored under the posts. It was a great try— the finest individual effort seen in Northern Union football for some time past. Leytham scored a try lafter clever work between himself and Jenkins, but up to the interval the play of the Northern Union side was disappointing. An injury to Eccles handicapped the side for a short time, but the Colonials continued to play a remarkably fine game, and Johnston and Todd scored. Messenger also kicked two goals. Towards the finish Eccles scrambled over theislevr Zealand line ibut in the end the Colonials won a great game by eighteen points to six. The Northern Union, team showed about the same form as they did when they won at-Leeds, but the New Zealanders' recovery after their appearance of stale- ness, was most surprising. The gate receipts amounted t'o £539.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON" A" V. LEIGH RANGEHS.—This match was played at Wilderspool on Saturday before a capital attendance. The home team was weakened by the demands of the first team at Bradford, and as a result the exhibition suffered. Warrington played against a stiff breeze during the first half, and the fine defensive work of Elliot Harris, who made a welcome re-appear- ance after a prolonged absence, was responsible for the persistent attack of the Leigh men being beaten off. Near the interval one of the Leigh forwards dropped a goal. In the second half the Warrington men early took the lead when Kenyon dribbled over, but from this point the play degenerated into a scramble, and the final score was :—Warrington' one try (three points) ; Leigh Rangers, one dropped goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
