        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR The Brewster Sessions.—An Appeal to , DEATH OF MR. R. A. NAYLOR 1 Temperance Workers. i NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. 1 Sir,—The proposal to transfer the Bridge Inn licence Authors of letters intended for publication must in to Gorsey-lane will in all probability be granted at the all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor adjourned Sessions unless the Temperance party bestir as a guarantee of good faith. themselves and organise opposition immediately. The holding of public meetings to demand local veto The Licensing Bill, becomes a farce when those who claim to lead the Sir,—lt is hoped the Imperial Government's coming Temperance party content themselves by passing Licensing Bill will contain a leaf from that of our own resolutions. A vigorous campaign should forthwith be Local Parliamentary Debating Society's and grant undertaken, every resident in the Gorsey-lane dis- powees to refuse a licence on petition from two-thirds trict should be canvassed, and a number of house- of the ratepayers of the ward. Certain individuals may holders should be asked to come to Court prate about the liberty of the subject being done away and declare their strong opposition to a huge with, but surely liberty is non-eat when a public-house drinking shop being thrust upon them. After this has been arranged the opposition should be profession- is dumped in your neighbourhood whether you want it or not. Liberty under existing conditions is denied ally represented at the Court. A determined effort is communities—let alone solitary individuals—as they being made to. push this thing through and it behoves every roan and woman who has the moral and social have no voice in the matter. This local option means welfare of the district at heart to make a supreme effort more local liberty. The Progressive Party must to strengthen the hands of those magistrates who are strengthen its stakes in regard to local option or strongly opposed to such a monstrous prottosal. popular control. They have powerful monopolies , The matter is so urgent and so vitally important that against them. The Brewer and the Bishop sometimes go together—two busy B's. I recollect a remark of a not a moment should be lost. certain Cabinet Minister to the effect that "the church's „.Temperance reformers organise, organiee, organise ! ' one foundation is beer barrels." This may COMO nearer , lou s, VIGILANCE. to the truth than some people are aware. "Up and doing"is the motto for Liberals just now. Apathy 1 retards l'eform.—Yoars &amp;c. 1 " Is Interest Robbery ? " ! Sir,—Mr. Parker is still very unfortunate in his 'Latchford. reply, which is quite as illogical as those which have _ Ile Town Council and Councillor preceded it. Ido not court a further charge of vague- ness, neither do I wish to offend Mr. Parker, but really Pemberton. —and his third attempt too—when he tells us that the case of the man who ' thinks out and makes a spade" Sir,--,A correspondent, writing over the name of is on all fours with the interest-monger,then indeed my -" Ratepayer" in one of your contemporaries last task does seem hopeless. Notwithstanding, I have the Saturday, directly insinuates that the so-called pleasure of informing tlr. Parker, and those who share "present " to Councillor Pemberton is to cost £lOO, his views—and doubtless there are many—that to "think whereas as be knows,it will not cost one farthing. The out and make a spade" involves both mental and Town Clerk holds three copies of the Lawson Contract manual labour, and as such is entitled to the reward of proceedings : he wants one copy for himself, and one ability, and generally gets it, sometimes more so. -for the Museum ; the Council have decided, by 19 Under our present system this reward usually takes the votes to 5, that the third copy shall be given to COun- form of royalty, rent, or ;hare of profits, and is a recog- cillor Pemberton. There is no doubt this decision has nition of the fact, tha