lor Pemberton had recom- , long before juggling and trucking with money was mended as the expert engineeer for the investigatlon of , thought of. Kettle, by thrift, industry, good luck, and the Lawson claim. Honour, therefore, to whom special traits of inheritance, saved 100 loads of potatoes. honour is due ! ; Observing the innocence and simplicity of his neigh- Doubtless credit is deserved by the officials who 1 bour Parker, he seized upon the idea of further enrich- loyally carried through Councillor Pemberton's policy. i ing himself at the latter 's eepense. Approaching Parker I refer to the Deputy Town Clerk, Mr. Hallaway, snd 'he offers to loan him the 100 loads of potatoes on the Deputy Water Engineer, Mr. Gray ; but these ' condition that he will return 103 or to be more exact, gentlemen are servants of the Council, and their work 1120 loads when next year's crops are harvested. Parker will no doubt receive due recognition. iin a weak moment accepts Kettle's offer. But when " Ratepayer's " attack on the Investigation Cons- the time arrives for the fulfilment of the bargain, mittee is as discreditable as his attempt to mislead , parker, by dint of hard work and much loss of sleep o' your readers in regard to the cost of the gift to Coun- I nights thinking, finally decides to repudiate the usury cillor Pemberton. The relatively insignificant expendi- ; part of the business and flatly tells Kettle that whereas ture of this Committee was the measure of the splendid he will have pleasure in returning the 100 loads actually work of Mr. Garside, C. E., whose exhaustive and con- , loaned, not one solitary potato° more will he part with. elusive report alone enabled the Corporation's repro- ; As to which of these two sinners is the greatest, he sentatives in the arbitration to triumph so signally as 1 who in his heart sought to commit usury upon his the speeches at the last Council Meeting plainly indicate. ; neighbour, or he who perjured himself rather than If" Ratepayer" did not know, then he ought to have j enslave himself, I will leave our Mr. Parker and your known, that the investigation committee withheld a readers to judge for themselves.—Yours truly, report because they were so advised in the interest of I RUPERT KETTLE. the Corporation's case. Personally I hope the Com- mittee will now issue their report. If they do' it will satisfy. " Ratepayer" in a way. that he will hardly appreciate, and it will vindicate in every respect every move and every act of Councillor Pemberton, and of the Non-trading Association in relation to this Lawson Contract matter. It would not have been" Ratepayer " had he not had a fling at Alderman Pierpoint, and at your humble servant, the Chairman of the Non-Trading. Association, and had he not sought to fulsomely champion his friend the Town Clerk. The latter no doubt appreciates the support of an apologist who dare not add his own name to his letter, and I am sure Alderman Pierpoint is well able to take care of himself should he deem it worth his while to honour by his notice his calumniator. So far as lam personally concerned, I am indifferent to the attacks of " Ratepayer " or of any other opponent who hides behind a pseudonym on a great public issue. As the representative however of the Non-trading Association„ Tam not at liberty to follow my own bent. But I need only deal with the meanness and the disingenuousness of " Ratepayer " in misrepresenting the nature of my opposition to the appointment of Mr. George Hill as arbitrator in January, 190 g. My objec- tion was only to the appointment of any arbitrator, while Mr. Garside was engaged upon his investigation' and this my letter clearly sets forth. Here is the letter, which was addressed to every member of the Council:— Dear Sir,</p>
        <p>It is with deep regret, which will be shared by our readers, that we announce the death of Mr. Robert Anderten Naylor, of Langford Park, Langford, Somerset, and formerly of Guerden Hall, 'Plrelwall, which took place at two o'clock on Monday afternoon after a painful illness. The deceased gentleman had resided at Langford about twelve months, having purchased the estate better known as Mendip Lodge I from the late owner, Mr. Benjamin Somers, J.P. The prorerty had been in possession of the same family I very many years. Mr. Naylor's taking up his residelice 'there was generally welcomed, and after settling diwn 'he began to spend urmey freely in improving the property. Had he been spared there is no doubt that he would have greatly enhanced the attractiveness of I this well known beautiful estate.</p>
        <p>During his absence from home before Christmas he become seriously ill and a physician was called in. Only as the result of careful medical attention and nursing was he enabled to undertake the return I journey and on reaching his home a relapse took place. I It was found that he was suffering from that dread malady, cancer on the liver, and although everything possible was done for him by his devoted sister, Miss , Naylor, Dr. Piner and trained nurses, he gradually became worse and all hope of recovery was abandoned some weeks ago. He bore his trial with great forti- tude and, says a correspondent, expressed many times his wish to die and be with his Lord and Master Whom he loved and served. The end came ,peacefully and without pain at the time above stated. Mr. Naylor's death has cast a gloom over the village and neigh- bourhood, for although practically a .stranger he had made many friends, and the general hope was that he would remain long in his new home and become more closely identified with the people of Langford. He was a man of high moral Christian character, and so devoted was he to all that was pure and good that his loss will be greatly felt and many will sorrow over him who knew him not a few months ago. The late Mr. Naylor was associated with Warring- ton practically all his life and was prominently identi- fied with local commercial affairs. He was a native of Grappenhall, where lie was born on November sth, 181 G. His father, the late Mr. John Naylor, came to this district from Walton-le-Dale, Preston, and was a successful business man. It was in 1873 that Mr. R. A. Naylor founded the firm which bears his name at Howley, to carry on the work of cabinet making and joinery. The business itself was not a new one. Inaugurated in the early sixties, it was in its beginnings on a very modest scale, and conducted in connection with a cotton mill, but when Mr. R. A. Naylor came into possession the cotton mill machinery was cleared out, and the whole building utilised as a woodworking factory. With creditable forethought Mr. Naylor secured sufficient land for possible future expansion, and the selection of the site has been amply justified by the results. Year by year the works have been extended until they fully occupy the 37,000 square yards of land so fortu- nately situated in close proximity to the Mersey. As the development went on, new branches of trade, each conducted with the same business acumen which achieved the success of the others, were acquired arid to-day the concern stands in the forefront of timber importers, merchants and manufacturers. After a continuous and prosperous career the business was on November Ist, 1905, converted into a private limited company. During the whole of its career it has found consistent employment for about 300 workpeople. Up to a few years ago, Mr. Naylor resided at Cuerden Hall, Thelwall, a beautiful mansion which was built by his father and enlarged by Mr. Naylor. Here it was his delight to dispense princely hospitality and many are the organizations which have been entertained in the stately ball and the picturesque grounds. Unfortunately trouble arose with regard to what became widely known as the " Thelwall nuisance " and after a prolonged controversy in which Mr. Naylor failed to obtain the remedy he desired, he felt compelled to leave Thelwall, much to the regret of his many friends, and took up his resi- dence at St. Michael's House, Egerton Gardens, London. He also spent a great deal of his time in North Wales, to which he was particularly attached, and about a year ago he purchased his new home in Somersetshire. The surroundings of dint demesne are, we understand, ideal and Mr. Naylor might naturally have looked forward to a long and happy retirement, but it was not to be. It is interesting to note that Mr. Naylor was one of the Warrington gentlemen who took an active part in the promotion of the Manchester Ship Canal, and in recognition of his work he received an illuminated letter of thanks reading :—" Manehester Ship Canal Company. Robert A. Naylor, Esq. With thanks for honorary services during the year 1887. Signed by Earl Egerton of Tattoo, chairman ; Sir Joseph C. Lee, deputy chairman." Mr. Naylor was an ardent teetotaller and temper- ance worker and had occupied prominent positions in connection with the Warringtori Temperance Federa- tion. He was one of the founders of the Warrington and District Band of Hope Union and became its first President, a position which he held up to a little over a year ago. During that period the Union underwent many changes, there being a difficulty at times to get men to work the organisation, and on one occasion it was allowed to lapse. Mr. Naylor's ardent zeal for the movement was always manifest, and his characteristic generosity has often been the moans of encouraging Band of Hope workers in Struggling Societies. About ten years ago, along with several others. Mr. Naylor was instrumental in reorganising the Warring- ton Band of Hope Union. Having invited the delegates of the Lancashire and Cheshire Baud of Hope Union to hold one of their quarterly meetings in Warrington, Mr. Naylor acted as host at the Parr Hall, delegates being present from nearly every town in Lancashire and Cheshire. This was a Red Letter Day for the cause in Warrington. A great meeting was held in the Parr Hall in the evening and addresses were delivered by Canon Hicks and others, Mr.Naylor being Chairman. The linking, up with the Counties Union proved that Union was indeed strength and the local organisation has never since lacked workers, with the result that the organisation has become a great influence for gocd in the town. At Thelwall Mr. Naylor founded a Temperance FLU which was a centre of much useful work.</p>
        <p>H. F'.</p>
        <p>13th Yebruary, 1908.</p>
        <p>LAWaON CONTRACT. We think we need only call your attention to the extraordinary resolution of the Water Committee, which appears in the Council Agenda for next Tuesday's meeting, to ensure your unhesitating and indignant decision to vote against its confirmation. The resolution which has been put forward at the instigation of the Town Clerk reads as follows : 'That the Committee consent to the appointment of Mr. George Hill, of Manchester, as arbitrator to settle the dispute between Mr. George Lawson and I the Corporation,and that an application be made to the County Court Judge for this appointment.' in view of the fact that the Council a short time ago specially appointed Mr. Garside, C.E. (who is au expert on the precise kind of work done by Mr. Lawson), to thoroughly investigate the enormous claims for extras put forward by the contractor, it I would not only be the height of folly, but an injus- tice and an insult to the ratepayers, to make an I appointment, oe take any steps to settle with Mr. ; Lawson, until Mr. Garside's report has been received, and we feel sure you will take care to be present at Tuesday's Council meeting to vote against the confirmation of the above resolution, the object of which must be very clear to you.—l am your obedient servant, for the Warrington Non-Trading Association. JAMES DODDS, Chairman." The result of this letter was that the resolution the Non-traders objected to was withdrawn even before the Council met. The reasonableness of their contention was apparent, and not a member of the thirty-three present had a single word to say in defence of the ' Town Clerk's action. Nevertheless an unwary reader i of" Ratepayer's" half-trw h might naturally conclude that I had opposed the appointment of Mr. George Hill at any time or under any circumstances. Let me add however that I am convinced that, had a pumping , expert of Mr. Garside's great and precise experience been the arbitrator, the town would have been saved a further £lO,OOO, as I happen to know that Mr. Garside's report clearly proved by alternative methods that the contract amount of £21,000 odd was the full value of the work necessary to be done. Many of us are as well aware almost as"Ratepaver" himself of the discreditable attempt made in connec-1 tion with a meeting of a public body held just before ; the last Council meeting, to induce Alderman Forshaw to withdraw his motion to present Councillor Pemberton with the copy of the Lawson Contract proceedings, or, as an alternative, to absent himself from the Council Meeting. We are glad Alderman Forshaw stuck to his guns with the approval of the members of the Council and to the great satisfaction of the public outside. Sir, it would have been wiser if Ratepayer " had "let sleeping dogs lie." The Non-traders were satisfied to have compelled the fight against Lawson's claim, and to have brought about so remarkable a victory and so great a saving to the town •, but the meanness of such men as" Ratepayer " tempts the Non-traders for the information of the public to recapitulate their original contentions and to point out ohne more the original moral. The illegal and immoral trading so rampant- and expanding 7 or 8 years ago was demoralizing the whole of our local public life ; the then Mayor and the then Chairman of the Water Committee were supplying Corporation contractors and Corporation committees directly or indirectly through companies they controlled and held controlling interests in, with large quantities of materials ; the officials who depended upon the Council for a livelihood—the servants indeed of these men—were in the position of having to pass the goods of their own employers. The contractors and all concerned saw all this, and so from top to bottom demoralization reigned, and we have seen as time has gone on the disastrous natural results, the latest a pathetic tragedy, which would never have been possible, and could never have occurred, except under the appalling condition of public life in Warring. ton which the Non-Traders impeached. and have ultimately overthrowp,--- un aro" JAMES DODDS.</p>
        <p>It was by his political fight in the Carnarvon Baroughs at the last General Election that Mr. Naylor come most prominently before the public. He was adopted as the Unionist candidate at a meeting held at Carnarvon on August Bth, 1901, and at once entered upon a vigorous compaign. A more formidable oppment than the sitting Member, Mr. D. Lloyd- George, he could not have had and the result - a great victory for Mr. Lloyd-George was a foregone con- clusion. Whilst in North Wales Mr. Naylor identified himself with much of the recreative life of the district and was a warm supporter of the musical festivals and the yachting clubs. One of Mr. Naylor's chief hobbies was literature and he wrote a great deal of poetry and prose. His works included : " Nugte Canoras," " Prince Oscar's Marriage Ode," "Shall we know them again ?" and "Sweden 1882, Norway 1883." He was the author of hundreds of hymns, and when he was in the habit of delivering his Sunday evening lectures at the Temperance Hall at Thelwall be wrote a hymn for each service, which was sung to a well-known tune. Mr. Naylor was a great collector of pictures, antiques, and curios, and prior to the recent sale, Cuerden Hall contained a most extensive collection gathered from all parts of the world. His London residence is also full of objects of interest. At the Bristol Art Gallery during the last few months there has been on view a very fine carpet lent by Mr. Naylor which was made to commemorate a Treaty of Commerce s;gned between Great Britain and France in 1860.</p>
        <p>I He was a great pedestrian, and in 1371, in company I with his brother, Mr. John Naylor, walked from 'John o'Groats to Land's End, without crossing a ferry !or riding in a vehicle of any description, the total ! distance covered being 1,360 miles, at an average of 25 miles per day. They .did not take a direct route, ! but often went across country, east and west, visiting any objects of interest they felt disposed to see. Ever ready to assist movements for the uplifting of the poorer classes, Mr. Naylor took a keen interest in the work of the Warrington Ragged School. As a matter of fact, he was its first supporter, for when Mr. Skelland opened the institution twenty-one years ago he met him very generously in the provision of the timber required for seating purposes. Mr. Naylor remained an annual subscriber to the school and it was his custom to give a treat to about 300 children there every year. He and Miss Naylor were very frequent and welcome visitors and on several occasions Mr. Naylor preached the annual sermons He will be greatly missed by the workers at the institution, who found in him a true friend. A staunch Churchman, Mr. Naylor was at one time actively associated with Thelwall Church. On removing to London he attended Holy Trinity Church, Brompton, of which the Rev. A. W. Gough is Vicar, and took such a great interest in</p>
        <p>February 14th, 1903.</p>
        <p>For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
