      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>INFIRMARY EXTENSION. Successful Juvenile Ball at the Farr Hall.</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY EXTENSION. Successful Juvenile Ball at the Farr Hall.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall presented a merry scene an Thurcday ' evening when a juvenile ball,in aid of the fund for pre. I viding a children's cot in the Infirmary extension, was held. The project had been enthusiastically taken in ; hand by a representative committee of ladies, with Mr. J. H. Smethurst as the energetic hon. secretary, and ' complete success crowned their efforts. About 210 I young people between the ages of 6 and 18 were present and spent a thoroughly enjoyable time. They were received by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) and then passed into the large ball where dancing was kept up with much zest from 7to 11 o'clock. Messrs. Crosflelds' band supplied an excellent selection of music. During intervals Mr. W. W. Whittle gave some ca.pital humorous songs, and a conjuring entertainment by " Young Marvelo " ; quite mystified the audience. It should be mentioned that the dance programme was embellished with a ' pleasing sketch of an urchin in a cot, which had been 1 drawn by Miss Florence Laverock. Refreshments were I served, the catering of the Winmarleigh Cafe Company I giving every satisfaction. The duties of stewards were I discharged by Mr. C. Plinston (M.C.), Mr. J. H. Smethurst, Captain Crosfield, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Mr. J. Richardson, jun., Mr. I. Sankey, Mr. F. V. L. Mathias, Dr. Bowden, Dr. Brown, Mr. W. Forshaw, Mr, Hedley Taylor, Mr. F. Wright, Mr. T. D. Amos, and Mr. J.H.J. Hampson. The following ladies rendered valuable assistance :—The Mayoress, the Misses Smethurst, Mrs. J.H.Smethurst,Mrs.Fox,Mrs.Bowden,Mrs.Lightburn,Mrs. Stevenson, Mrs. Monks, Mrs. Buckton, Mrs. Densham, I Mrs. Bracecamp, Mrs. Sankey, Misses Sankey, Mrs. Mathias' J. ' Mrs. F. J Lord Misses Spinks Miss Williams' ! Misses Forshaw, Miss Frost, Miss Campbell, Miss 1 Warburton, Mrs. Ormandy, Miss Houghton, Mrs. 'Harold Jolley, Mrs. John Evans, Miss Lomas, Miss I Burton, Mrs. Short, Mrs. F. Parsonage. I The Mayoress was presented with a beautiful bouquet on behalf of the ladies' committee, by her little grand- daughter, Margaret Ellen Smethurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CHILD'S SUSPICIOUS DEATH.</head>
        <p>CHILD'S SUSPICIOUS DEATH.</p>
        <p>-- Inquest Adjourned.</p>
        <p>THE CORONER AND THE NEW LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday, Nlr.S. Brighouse opened an inquest on the body of William Glover, six weeks old son of Arthur Glover, wireworks labourer, 3, Brackley-street. The Coroner stated that the mother of the child was addicted to drink, and Inspector Cox, of the N.S.P.C.C. had had the case under observation for some time. On the 6th inst. he served her with a final warning before prosecution. On the day the child died the mother said she got up at 7.45, washed it, and put it back to bed, and went up several times to attend to it, but at 12 noon found it dead. When the police made inquiries she was found to be suffering from the effects of drink, and unable to explain properly what occurred lon the morning of the death of the child between 8.20 and 12. Dr. Smith, police surgeon, was 'called in. and he told him that he saw the j child when it was four days old, but not since. Jln these circumstances he (the Coroner) thought it to be his duty to have a post-mortem examination made so 1 that all the facts could be put before the jury to enable ' them to decide whether the mother had done anything to bring about the death of the child. The father was a respectable, hard-working man. 1 The father having given formal evidence of the death, I Mrs. Glover was put into the box, and advised by the Coroner to obtain legal assistance at the adjourned injury. He reminded her that on the Bth January of last yegr he held an inquest on another son, Stanley I Glover, and that the next day she was brought before the Bench and sent to prison for three months for I neglect. The Coroner added that the Legislature had I made up their minds to see that child life was protected, and he was glad to find that one of the most sensible Bills he had yet heard of was introduced into the 'House of Commons on the previous night with that j object in view. They would have no more over-lying lin bed and of children being burnt to death in conse- quence of unprotected fires. 1 Glover said his wife had been warned about her con- duct to her children.</p>
        <p>------- --- The Coroner: They tell me you are a sober, hard- working man. Glover: I have given her all the advice I possibly could. -</p>
        <p>The Coroner : But she won't take it. The inquiry was then adjourned until Monday.</p>
        <p>For Children's Hacking Coughs at night, Woods (Iraq Peppermint Cure. 1/1i 219.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
