      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 15, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 15, 1908.</p>
        <p>it that he became Vicar's warden. He also devoted considerable time to mission work in the poorer dis- tricts. He was a great lover of musi3, and it is a pathetic circumstance that on January 30th he was to have presided at a musical festival in London He was of a kindly, affable nature, ever ready to extend his sympathy and help to less fortunate brethren, and he will be greatly missed by a large circle. To his sister, Miss Naylor, who was his constant companion, the deepest sympathy will go forth in her heavy bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Funeral.</head>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>The large attendance at the funeral,which took place in the peaceful churchyard at Grappenhall at noon yesterday, testified to the universal respect entertained for the deceased gentleman. The body was brought to Warrington by train on Thursday evening, leaving Langford at 2 o'clock and arriving here at 7.33, and was immediately conveyed to Cuerden Hall, Thelwall, where it remained over-night. The funeral yesterday was of the simplest description, the coffin being carried by bearers from Cuerden Hall to Grappenhall, and the whole of the mourners following on foot. At the head of the cortege walked 300 of the employees lof Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., the works at Howley having been closed for the day. The old church in which the first part of the burial service was held, was crowded, and the proceedings were deeply impressive. The choir was in attendance and the hymn They whose course oa earth is o'er " was sung. The officiating clergy were the Rev, 11, V. Pigot (Rector of Grappenhall), the Rev. T. Pym Williamson (Vicar of Thelwall), and the Rev. Canon E. 'V. Davies, residentiary canon of Bangor Cathedral. Mr. Walter Wood presided at the organ and as the mourners left the church he played Chopin's majestic Funeral March." The last rites at the graveside were performed by the Rev. H. V. Pigot, and at the close the hymn " Let saints on earth in concert sing " was sung. The chief mourners wore:—Mr. John Naylor (brother), Messrs. James Anderton Naylor, Henry Anderton Naylor, H. J. S. Anderton and A. Anderton, (nephews). The directors of Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Limited in attendance were : Messrs. L. Henshall, J. Astley, S. Shakeshaft, and J. Edwards (secretary). The repre- sentatives of the employees present included :—Ottice staff : Messrs. E. Howard, W. Cookson, R. P. Williams, W. J. Stewart, G. Whitehouse, J. Daintith, M. Owen, J. W. Gibbs, A. B. alwards,J. Metcalf, and A. Ellison; foremen, Messrs. W. H. Pearce. W. W. Roberts, T. Hunt, I. Lawson, W. Dalatith, D. Davies G. James ; travellers, Messrs. E. R. Putton, R. Pierce, J. Astley, James Astley, sen., E. Hayes, T. S. Hartley, E. Jones, J. B. Brand, W. E. Jones; Walton employees, Mr. J. Hourie.</p>
        <p>Firms in the timber trade were represented as follows :—Messrs. Thomas Hughes and Son (Liverpool) and Sir Thomas Hughes, Mr. W. G. Whiteman ; Messrs. Watson and Todd (Liverpool), Mr. Edward McGowan ; Messrs. James Halsall and Son (Liver- pool), Mr. A. Halsall • Messrs. Thomas Leicester and Co. (Liverpool), Mr. T. C. Johnson ; Messrs. Henry Quayle and Sons (Liverpool), Mr. W. H. Quayle ; Messrs. Harrison and Robinson (Liverpool), Mr. Joseph Robinson; Messrs. Lumley, Lloyd and Co. (Liverpool), Mr. R. Lumley ; Messrs. R. Cox and Co. (Liverpool), Mr. Wishart; Messrs. Tealby and Co. (Hn11), Mr. Dixon.</p>
        <p>, The„ Warrington Band of Hope Union was represented by Messrs. T. Dale and G. Whitfield, and the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Elope Union by Mr. Wilfred L. Eddy. Councillor Skelland and Nlr. S.Gough represented the Warrington Ragged School and Mission. The following employees at Cuerden Hall were present-:—Messrs. F. Leah, L Searle, J. Laurie, L. Leah.</p>
        <p>Amongst others present were :—The Mayor of War- rington (Alderman Smethurst), Alderman Forshaw, Captain Waring, Rev. J. F. Matthews, Messrs. *. G. Groves (Lyrarri), Joseph Bleach (St. Helens), W. Harold Webb (Bristol), Mr. J. T. Smith (agent to Lord Egerton of Tatton), and Mrs. Smith, W. Owen, W. Skinner, James Henshall (Chester), A. Lister-Kaye and Leigh Leyland (churchwardens of Grappenhall), H. Leyland, F. Hickman, T. Lloyd, A. Wright. T. HArding (Acton Grange), J. T. Haswell, of Chester (auditor to Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd.), S. Saxon Buten (St. Michael's) ; Mr. R. M. Prescott, Town Clerk of She:aeld, and Mr. Tatham, of Messrs. Tatham and Worthington, Man- chester (the deceased gentleman's executors). Mr. W. Cbandos Wilson (secretary of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of-Hope Union) and the Rev. Canon Stevenson were unable to be present. The following acted as bearers :—Employees of Howley Mills, Messrs. W. Daintirh, T. Hunt, J. Collin, J. Templeton, J. Walsh, A. Wilmhurst, T. Egerton, D. Davies, T. James, G. Rowley, T. McLellan, A. Dutton; Cuerden Hall employees, Messrs. F. Leah, J. Lawie, A. Wright, J. Scads, G. Astell, and E. Hewitt.</p>
        <p>Floral tributes were forwarded by the following : Miss Naylor (" to Robert from his devoted sister,") nephews and nieces, Mr. and Mrs. Barton, Miss Birton and Miss May Barton (Chappells), Mr. and Mrs. W. A. Lloyd, Mr. and Mrs. Lee (Carnarvon), Mrs. Grimshaw (Newholme, Ashton-on-Mersey), Mr. and Airs. Skinner and family, Mr. and Mrs. Shakeshaft, Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw, Mr. and Mrs. Prescott (Sheffield), Nurse Preston (Wrea Green), Hr. and Mrs. W. H. Prescott (Tottenham), Or. and Mrs. Marshall (Pendennis, Isle of Man), Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Smith (Kuutsford), " his godson, Eddie Sergeant," Dr. Edward Sergeant and Mrs. Sefgeant (Wrea Green), Mr. and Mrs. G. ['hatcher and family ( La ugfo rd), Mr. and Mrs. Lawrence (The Garde us, Mendip Park), Canon and Mrs. Davies (Bangor), " Roberts and Annie", Mr. and Mrs. Williams, Mrs. R. T. Fairclough (Barryfield, Latchford), Miss Shaw and members of the Howley Mothers' Mission, Rev. and Mrs. C. A. Griffin (Dunham Hill, Chester), " Searle, Cumin and Jim," Rear Admiral J. P. Jones. Parry and Mrs. Parry, Mrs. Lawie and family, Mr. and Mrs.Tiglae(Altrincham),Misses Ditchfield(Grappenhall), Miss Dutton (Conway), Mis. Walker Lucas (Crinkle- wood, London), Sydney and Leonard Henshall, Mr. H. Roberts, Councillor J. Skelland, Mr. Samuel Gough, Mr. D. Morris, Mr. and Mrs. Plicenix, directors of R. A. Naylor, Limited, the office staff and workmen at Hoivley Mills, the foreman, chairman and members of Executive Committee of the CArnaryonshire Constitu- tional Association, Carnarvon Habitation of the Prim- rose League, Carnarvon Workingmen's Club, Bangor Workingmen's Conservative Association, the servants at Cuerden Hall, the servants and tenantry on Nlendip Park Estate, Somerset, tenants of Caerden Cottages, Warrington Band of Hope Union, Committee of the Warrington Ragged School Mission,"rwo little boys in loving memory of dear genty." On the wreath sent by Mrs. Walker Lucas was a card with the words: "His life was gentle ; and the elements so inix'd in him That Nature might stand up and say to all the world This was a man.' ",</p>
        <p>The faneral arranieMent-s-W-ei;-Carried out at Lang- ford by Nlessrs. Laverton and Webb and at Warrington by Mr. Edward Connor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. ARTHUR A. DIGN UM.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. ARTHUR A. DIGN UM.</p>
        <p>The news will be received with deep regret of the death on Thursday, the Gth inst., of the Rev. Arthur A. Dignutn, a missionary who was well known to many in Warrington. He was formerly a member of Wycliffe Church, and in 1876 became a student of Lancashire Independent College, with the view of becoming a missionary in India. In 1882 he was ordained as a missionary under the London Missionary Society, and was stationed first at Gooty, in South India. Very early in his career at this station, how- ever, he was bereaved of his young wife, and lie found it desirable to change his sphere of labour. He removed, therefore, to Salem, where he continued to work, with manifold tokens of success for a long number of years. Here he married his second wife, and two daughters were born to him. Owing to serious failure in her health, his wife, a few years ago, came home to England, where she died in September, 1003. There is no doubt that this severe domestic sorrow, thus sadly repeated, had serious effect upon Mr. Dignum's health. Ile , continued, however, to carry on his work at Salem until about two years ago, when owing to changes in his views, he retired from connection with the London Missionary Society. He then sought and obtained ordination from the Bishop of Bombay as a clergyman of the Anglican Church, in the same province as that i he had laboured in for so many years, but n a some- what distant part of it. He was on furlough in England in one of the latter months of last year, and visited many of his old Warrington friends, spending some time in Penketh with Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker. He then appeared to be in good health and spirits. On returning to India, however, at the end of the year he had a severe attack of cholera Ivhich terribly weakened him, and sadly shattered his nervous system. By the congregation of Wycliffe Church in particular his death will be sincerely regretted, for he always occupied a warm place in their affections. The news of Mr. Dignum's death was reported to the Directors of the London Missionary Society on the 11th inst., and was received with very sympathetic interest. Dr. Wardlaw Thompson was visiting Salem a little more than a year ago and heard a good deal of Mr. Dignum's work in that Mission, and all he heard was of the most friendly and gratefal kind. Dr. Thompson says that "the mere fact that he dropped away from us towards the end of his life, on grounds which to some seemed insufficient, cannot affect the personal estimate of him as one who for many years was a very strong and able member of the Mission, and who, to the end of his life, was deeply interested in the people among whom he had laboured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LICENSING QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE LICENSING QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A Forecast of the Government Bill.</p>
        <p>The Licensing Bill for England and Wales, which Mr. Aspith is to pilot through the House of Commons will it is expected be introduced to Parliament next week. Already a hostile agitation has been set on foot, and there is every indication that the proposals of the Government will be met with stubborn opposi- tion not only from the brewers and publicans, but from the great majority of the Conservative party. It is a national misfortune that one of the great parties in the State should to so great an extent have allied itself with the makers and purveyors of alcoholic drinks. Individually, many Unionist politicians pro- fess to be interested in temperance reform, but when specific proposals are made by a Liberal Government the party as a whole rallies to</p>
        <p>THE DEFENCE OF THE BREWER AND rtrnicAs with as much zeal as for religious instruction in day schools or the union of Church and State. Hitherto only a small minority of Conservatives have taken an independent line, and it would seem prabable that in the coining debates not more than half-a-dozen will range themselves on the side of the Government in their attempt to limit the acknowledged evils of the present licensing system. The necessity for prompt legislation is mainly due to the A.ct of 1904, which for the first time gave a general recognition to the pernicious doctrine that an annual license-holder has a property in his license. The year before that unfortunate piece of legislation hr. Balfour received a deputation from the licensed trade and,in reply to the representations made to him, avowed himself a believer in the principle of compen- sation and promised to deal with the subject in a Government Bill. ljp to that time licensing justices had the power, when they found too many licenses in a district to refuse to renew any certificate which they considered unnecessary. This was a perfectly reasonable law, seeing that every license was granted for one year only, and that the small license fee was in no sense a quid pro qao for the grant of such a monopoly. A claim to compensation would have been reasonable, if on the grant of a new license, the applicant had been required to pay a substantial capital sum for the privilege conferred upon him, but as a matter of fact the value of the premises was I enormously enhanced by the action of the licensing lauthority and the whole of this increased value went into the pockets of the property-owner.</p>
        <p>ML BALFOUR S BILL was opposed to the utmost by the Liberal minority in the last Parliament and it was made clear by Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman and other Statesmen that the question would be re-opened whenever the party returned to power. Temperance reformers expressed some disappointment that the question was not dealt 'with last year,- but the Government programme was so long that it was found necessary to defer legislation until 1903. Now, however, it is to have a leading place on the order book, and there is little doubt that it will be sent to the House of Lords for consideration at an early stage of the Session. The defenders of</p>
        <p>TILE CLIUt TO COMPENgATIOY largely rest ‘their case on two considerations —the first that the compensation fund is contributed by the trade, and the second that the practical result is to accelerate the withdrawal of licenses. Both of these arguments are plausible, but a little reflection will show that they are delusive. With regard to the first it is plain that the charge upon the trade for compensation —which is really of the nature of a tax —diminishes the taxable capacity of licensed houses, and thus tends to dry up a potential source of revenpe. This point may be driven home when we remind our readers that for many years past there have been complaints that the present scale of annual licenses Is inequitable, and in the case of houses of higher value should be substantially increased. For example, a house of the annual value of £6O is required to "Pay a license fee of £25, but a London gin palace of the annual value of £l,OOO and doing probably fifty times as much business is let off with a fee of only £OO. Obviously this absurd disproportion needs rectifying, but if Mr. Asquith should propase an addition to the licence fee of houses of higher value, he will be at once reminded of-the compensation levy, and Parliament may be expected to take it into con- sideration in fixing the revised scale. It is plain, therefore, that the effect of a compensation fund drawn from the trade is to limit the taxable capacity of licence holders.</p>
        <p>With regard to the second plea which is urged in favour of the Act of 1904, it is, of course, admitted that there has been a considerable reduction in the number of licensed houses' but it must not be for- gotten that under the old law a steady reduction was going on, and that, apart from the action of licensing authorities, the licenses of a number of unprofitable houses were allowed to lapse. Now, the first effect of recognising a right to compensation is to give</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>_A FICTITIOUS vArrz</head>
        <p>_A FICTITIOUS vArrz</p>
        <p>to houses scarcely paying their way in decadent districts. Before the Act of 1904 was passed the owners in such districts often found it to their advantage to diminish their expenses by a policy of concentration, but to-day an unreinunerative house is kept open until it is selected for compensation. We do not pretend to sty how many of the 3,600 houses with on-licenses to which c onapensation has been awarded belong to this class, but the number is unquestionably large, and seriously discounts the claim made by the apologists for Mr. Balfour's Act. , Indeed; there is much to be said for the contention that by interfering with the economic law under which unprofitable licences lapsed from year to year, the Licensing Act of the late Government has been actually mischievous in operation. Other houses which have been bought out have chiefly been of 164 r value, and if the amount of their trade is taken into consideration, the reduction in the number of houses is much less important than at first appears. Another objection to the Act is that the compensation fund is quite inadequate to the purpose assigned it, the result being that in many populous centres the licensing authority has its hands tied aad is unable to diminish the number of houses because there are no available funds for compensation. Before the Act of 1901 was passed the magistrates were able t) consider applications for the renewal of licenses upon their merits, and in the years 190'3 and 1904 there was an average reduction of 614 annually without compensation. With such facts on record legislation is urgently called for, and the Government are entitled to the support not only of the temperance reformers, but of all,who are interested in the inde- pendence of our local authorities. At recent meetings of the licensed trade it has been urged with much vehemence that to fix a time linait will be to inflict an injustice on an important class of traders and to jeopardise</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>INVESTUENTS IN BREWERY SHIRES</head>
        <p>THE INVESTUENTS IN BREWERY SHIRES</p>
        <p>and public-house property. In this connection it is estimated that the value of licensed property in the United Kingdom is £200,030,00D and that a good deal of the capital has been provided by withws and other small investors. Obviously, however, an issue of this kind must be considered on public grounds, and what- ever sympathy may be felt for individuals who have been unwise enough to buy brewery shares, the ques- tion must be decided not by the sentiment of pity, but on grounds of public right. And here the Govern- ment is on strong ground, for since the ,decision of Sharp v. Wakefiehl there has been no shadow of manse for investlre rug trding brewery shares or similar securities as a safe investment. And it must be remembered that most of the great colt panies have been formed since then, and in some instaaces with the express though unavowed object of realising a property of declining value and doubtful permanence. Or to put it still more plainly, in not a few instances brewera have either sold out, or put themselves in the way of selling out, because they believed the high-water mark had been reached and that there was a probability of a gradual decline in the worth of their property. If this be so, and it seems to us indisputable, then it is idle to contend that investors who have accepted the risks of a threatened industry have even an equitable claim to compensation if their investment turns out a bad one. And, so far as the Act of 1904 is concerned, the brewers and their friends have surely not forgotten that the Liberal party protested against Mr. Balfour's Act andgave due notice that at the earliest oppor- tunity it would attempt its repeal. That hour has now come and the Government will certainly not be deterred from its purpose by the special pleading of either brewers or financiers.</p>
        <p>'dwell in the palace and mansion, My name's on the lips of the Royal, I am crowned with a million laurels By the hard-worn sons of toil ; I am hailed with a spirit of gladness In the homes of the rich and poor; For I'm known to the nations forever As Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
