      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>"UNDER THE RED ROBE." WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY'S</head>
        <p>"UNDER THE RED ROBE." WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY'S</p>
        <p>PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>The Dramatic Society has every title to credit and distinction for the performances of " Under the Red Robe" which have been witnessed at the Royal Court Theatre this week. In almost all respects the produc- tion of this romantic play has been such as to fully maintain the high repatation of the Society, and add to the laurels of its most prominent members. We do not consider "Under the Red Robe " to be a great play, and it hardly does justice to Mr. Stanley Weyman's stirring novel, but it presents an interest- ing , and picturesque sequence of events in that naemor- , abll period of France's history when the great Cardinal Richelieu was king in all but name. It !appears to us a defect in the play that the part allotted the Cardinal is so small as to afford little opportunity of pourtraying at all effectively the remarkable man who controlled both Church and State in France during the first half of the 16th century. Richelieu might have j been made a much more outstanding figure witla.advan- I tage to the dramatised version of the story. The lead is entirely in the hands of Gil de Berault, soldier of fortune and swordsman, and Renee de Coche- foret, to the comparative extinction of the many • subsidiary but essential characters. It has struck us more than once ghat the tendency of modern play- wrights to centre almost all the interest upon the hero and heroine of a romance is a mistake, and nowhere jis this more emphasized than in an amateur produc- tion where one wishes to see full scope for the talent ' which the Society possesses in its members: To all intents and purposes,after de Berault and Renee have gathered in the encomiums which are their due, there are only a few crumbs of credit left for the rest of the company—and solely ' because opportunity is denied them by the limitations of the play. In brief, the story relates how Gil de Berault forfeits his life by trangressing the Cardinal's edict against duelling and is given one more chance to redeem himself by compassing, the capture of Henri. de j, Cocheforet, a Huguenot leader. Gil de Berault,under the assumed name of de Barthe, obtains the entree to ' the Chateau de Cocheforet and wins the confidence of Madame and her sister-in-law, Renee de Cocheforet. I While pursuing his distasteful commission de Berault's better nature comes to the front, and we see him torn I between his sense of duty to the Cardinal and his j honour as a gentleman of France. The love of Ren6e finally turns the scale and de Berault releases his prisoner, and goes back to the Cardinal prepared to rorfeit the life he now holds doubly dear. Fortunately, the Cardinal appreciates the heroism of the man and j the play has a happy denouement. I The scenes, and the cast of the play, are as 110110 w :</p>
        <p>Act I. Zaton's Eating-house in Paris "Marked Cards " 'Act 11. Room in Chateau de Cocheforet "The Spy" Act ILI. Scene I. Garden of Chateau. " From Darkness to Daybreak " Scene 2.—The Cross Roads Act IV. Scene I.—An Inn on the Road to Paris. Scone 2. The Great Gallery in the Cardinal's Palace. • " The Day of Dupes " CHARACTERS. . _ _</p>
        <p>Gil de Berault Mr. Charles H. Wood Cardinal Richelieu ' Mr. H. G. Mosley Marquis de Pombal Mr. Victor Roberts De Fargis Mr. R. P. Johnson Captain Larolle Mr. Sidney Wellhead The Lieutenant Mr. W. Owen Smith Sir Thomas Brunt Mr. H. Chambers Clan . Mr. H. G. Mosley Louis Mr. J. Craitc • Sergeant Mr. Harvey Adams Henri de Cocheforet Mr. Victor Roberts Renee de Cooheforet Mrs. Robert Garnett Madame de Cocheforet Miss Bessie Pierpoint Madame Zaton Miss Mary Brundrett Suzette Miss Rose Wellhead I Miss Wellhead Waitresses f Miss Bessie Richardson</p>
        <p>I We have nothing but tile highest praise for the acting of Mrs. R. Garnett is the fine part of Renee de Cocheforet. She has undoubtedly added another to Ithe striking list of Successes which have fallen to her , art in recent years, and whether in the lighter passages with the amusing Captain Larolle or in the trying situations with de Berault, she is equally artistic and convincing. Mr. C. H. Wood can be honestly credited ; with having perfected one of the best studies he has yet attempted, in the role of Gil de Berault. For a I man of the hero's type a distinctive stage presence is essential and Mr. Wood makes a capital swaggering ; bravo. But the character of the duellist has to be ; dropped at the Chateau do Cocheforet, and ia the dramatic situations with which he is faced here, Mr. Wool rises to heights which even his warmest admirers would hardly have expected. A comparatively small one is the second ladies' part,Madarne de Cache- . foret,but Miss Bessie Pierpoint endows it with grace and charm. One always looks to Mr. H. G. Mosley for a finished piece of acting, and in the two roles of the Ca'rdinal, and Clan, a dumb servant at Cocheforet,. he distinguishes himself with very scant opportunity. His Richelieu is impressive, and one regrets that the play does not permit us to see more of the great churchman. Dual roles also fall to the lot of Mr. Victor Roberts—the Makuis de Pombal and Henri de Cocheforet. As the former he is thoroughly cavalier- like, but as the Huguenot leader he cuts rather an insignificant figure. This is largely again the fault of the plarwright, but Mr. Roberts might with advan- tage imbue Henri with a little more of the spirit and demeanour which would make him worthy the devotion of Madame and Renee de Cocheforet. There is only one touch of humour in the play, and this falls to Mr. Sidney Wallhead to provide as the boastful but effeminate Captain Larolle. He skilfully enacts the dandy officer and provokes much mirth with his mock heroics. It is an open question, however, whether Mr. Wallhead does not exaagqerate the mincing gait of the "gallant " Captain. The Lieutenant who does the real work f)r which Captain Larolle 'claims the credit is effectively played by Mr. W. Owen Smit'a In the first act Miss Mary Brundreti appears in the part of Madame Zaton, and evinces not a little of the ability of her more experienced Sister, Mrs. Garnett. Mr. H. Chambers makes his debut with the Society as Sir Thomas Brunt, a not very heroic part, but one into which he might throw a little more energy with advantage. Minor characters are creditably inter- preted by Miss Rose Wallhead, Mr. R. P. Johnson, Mr. Harvey -Adams, Miss Wallhead and Miss Bessie Richardson.</p>
        <p>We should add that the play is splendidly mounted and richly dressed, the production in these important respects being a distinct credit to the Society and those who have assisted. During the I last scene between Richelieu and de Berault, a 'Gregorian chant is heard in the distance, the music for which was arranged with notable skill by Mr. J. Eckersley, assisted by Mr. F. Fieldhouse and party. "Under the Red Robe" has been played every night this week Co crowded audiences, and a sub- stantial sum should be raised for the Infirmary Extension fund and the Children's Summer Camp Fund. The concluding performance will be given this evening. A word of praise is due to Mr. J. W. Millen, the Hon. Secretary to the sosiety, for the admirable arrangements which have contributed so much to the general excellence of the week's work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WycLltf P.S. A.</head>
        <p>WycLltf P.S. A.</p>
        <p>At an open meetitig of the Wycliffe Society held on Sunday, Councillor Hawthorn, who presided, made reference to the assassination of the King of Portugal and the Crown Prince, and the motherly behaviour of the Queen in trying to protect her son. He said the deed was a blow to dictatorship, which would not be allowed in a democratic country. The Rev. J. F. Matthews gave an address entitled " On sale," in which, with examples from ancient and Biblical history, as well as upon modern experience,he showed the power of man and urged all present to display the true qualities of manhood. He had a contempt for any man who would sell his vote for a pint of beer, or withdraw from service because some offence had been given him. They ought to serve God because of His great love for us, and not from fear of hell or torment. Mr. J. B. Poole sane in good style " 0 God have mercy" and " Thou'rt passing hence." Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. Beswick also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>Marriages. BRIMELOW--HARDMAN—Febnutry Bth, at the Parish. Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, Henry. Brimelow to Jane Hardman. BELL—WHlTE—February Bth, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, Alfred Bell to Catherine White. Deaths. NAYLOR.—On Fenuary 10th at his Somersetshire • residence, Mendip Park, Langford, Robert Anderton I Naylor of Cuerden Hall, Thelwall, near Warrington MR. MARSHALL HALL, tC., AND A LIVERPOOL in his 62nd year. at Tuticorin, South SEAT. Drorwm—Oh the 6th February India, the Rev. Arthur A. Dignum, aged 51 years. Mr. Marshall Hall, rV.C., was on Saturday night By cable. adopted as Unionist candidate for the East l'osteth Division of Liverpool. At the last election there was ,no opposition, to the sitting member. Mr. Austin Taylor, who gaite no pledge except that he was opposed to tarif reform, the promotion of which Mr. Marshall , Hall now makes the principa! plank of his political platform. Mr. Taylor has since joined the Liberal Party.</p>
        <p>Jarrington.—Printed and published by JOHN WALE &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing Works- Bewsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road WIDNES 32, George-street, Sr. Lietess: 3, Market Street, EARLgsrowN; Church-street RUNCORN Feb. 15th 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-02-15_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>By " Ovallst." Warrington fell gently on Saturday at Bradford, and were not seriously hurt. They could hardly be expected to win with only two of the regular backs doing duty, but they made a good fight and actually held the lead at half-time. Then an injury to P. Boardman deprived the side of this promising for- ward's service; and the Bradford men soon had the match in their hands, the final score being 12 points to 4. Thus in two successive games, Warrington have failed to cross their opponents' line. Prior to the Salford match they had scored at least one try in every match this season. Their defensive record is still second only to Oldham's.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington go to Wigan and next Saturday Wigan come to Warrington. These two games are of vital importance to both clubs. The South West Lancashire League Championship will be definitely settled, and the cup will have to leave Runcorn for either Wigan or Warrington. Wigan have the best chance, for they have only to beat Warrington to-day and thesup is won. In order for Warrington to have a chance they must either draw or win to-day—the latter preferably, and then make no mistake about the following week's engagement, and the home match with St. Helens. Personally, Ido not fancy Warring- ton's chance, but with Jenkins and Leytham away from the Wigan side to-day there is room, for hope. The Wire-pullers will have to show much smarter form than they have done of late, and a win for them at Wigan is a rarity.</p>
        <p>-- The South West Lancashire League table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>Points. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pte Wigan Runcorn 9 ... 5 4 ... 0 ... 70 68 10 Warrington Leigh St. Helens 8 ... 2 ... 5 ... 1 ... 41 ...121 5 Widnes</p>
        <p>The Warrington team should be more representa- tive than it was a we,-1 ago, though valuabla men like Jenkins, Lees, P. Boardman and Taylor are still incapacitated By the way, all Warrington sportsmen sympathise with Ike Taylor who, in addition to being ill, has this week lost his brother A. Taylor (of Widnes) by a terrible works fatality. Young Alf Taylor was one of the best of the Widnes backs and he will be much missed by his club mates. Warrington I expect to have Isherwood, Dickenson and Brooks in the field again to-day.</p>
        <p>-- The selected team is: Tilley, full back ; Fiala Isherwood, Dickenson, and Watkin threequar.ers ; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs; Shugars, A. Boardman, F. Jenkins, Jolley, Belton, and Harmer forwards.</p>
        <p>The Wigan team will be: Sharrock, full back ;- Miler, Treharne, T. Thomas and Creevey, three- quarters ; David and J. Thomas, half backs ; Rams. dale, Willcocks, Barton, Brooks, Cheatham and Silcock, forwards.</p>
        <p>Almost as much interest centres in the " A" team matches between the two clubs, for the juniors are running neck and neck for the Combination champion- ship, with Warrington first favourites. They mast get the better of the Wiganers to make sure, for they have yet to visit Leigh Sharurocks,'Pemberton, and Wigan Highfield.</p>
        <p>The "A" team will be : T. Knight, full back ; Landor, Kenyon, Jordan and Tinkler, threequarters ; Daint,ith and Forster half backs; Naylor, Holbrook, Morley, Kettle, Fairhurst and Bradshaw forwards.</p>
        <p>The Northern League table up-to-date isva follows: For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'ge. Oldham E:lnnslet Wigan 23 16 6 1 55 91 383 28 31 149 71'73 Salford Broughton Keighley 23 15 8 0 46 52 248 40 34 182 65•21 Hull K. R. Batley 24 15 9 044 55 253 35 49 217 62.50 Halifax Warrington Wakefield T 23 13 9 1 49 72 314 42 64 246 58.69 Hull 25 14 11 0 48 61 288 34 39 185 56.00 Bradford 24 13 11 0 40 50 230 32 64 256 54.16 Runcorn ...... Barrow Rochdale Dewsbury 22 9 13 0 38 41 199 34 52 224 4010 Leeds Huddersfield Leigh Merthyr Swinton Ebbw Vale 2O 5 14 1 15 23 99 38 62 262 2759 York 22 6 16 0 33 37 177 58 64 308 27.51 St. Helens 23 5 16 2 27 33 153 43 95 371 2608 Widnes 2l 3 15 3 16 32 128 38 61 259 2142 Brainley</p>
        <p>I was glad to see that the All Blacks did themselves justice in the Chelsea match and fairly trounced the Northern Union in the second test. It is, perhaps, a bit unfortunate that the N.U. should cut up so roughly when they were on a sort of missionary exhibition, but the tourists have been so unfortunate that no one can grudge them the victory, especially after the brilliant display they gave. I have all along been convinced that, at their best, the All Blacks are a cleverer side than any N.U. combination. We have no men in the same class—now that Fish is out of the running—as Smith, Messenger and Todd.</p>
        <p>Some of the London papers were rather half-hearted in their comments on the test match at Chelsea, but not so the "Morning Leader" writer " Stealaway who fairly lets himself go: in praise of the game Says ho " The difference between the Rugby ad the Northern Union game is as great as that bgtween a youthful playwright's effort and the finished Gaiety production. The Iforthern Union have frozen-on to the best parti of the Rugby game, added a few gags of their o an, and blue-pencilled the stage-waits ' parts. The result is a game tremendously fast and interesting to watch."</p>
        <p>• "The All Blacks won because they played up to their best form. I cannot recall ever seeing two better full-backs opposed to one another than Taylor and Turtill. Jolley, of Runcorn is a great little half, but I should pick out Wrigley, at five eighths, as the best man on the field. He is a strong runner, fine kick, and fear-nothing tackler."</p>
        <p>The third and deciding" test" takes place to-day at Cheltenham,and the N.U. Committee has astounded the football world withy the drastic changes in their team. I do not suppose the fresh selections will make much difference either way, as so many of our men are on a par. But an injustice has certainly been done to Dickenson, who while being first reserve all ,through, has seen Llewellyn and P. Thomas in succession promoted over his head. The Warrington Committee are going to play Dickenson at Wigan. to-day, unless one of the selected quartette for Cheltenham cries off. It will be noted that Thomaa • , has not kept his place, and is again on the reserve. I The Welshman had not been keeping in the best condition lately, and his play has suffered.</p>
        <p>-- The Northern Union side is : Back, H. Taylor (Hull). Three-quarter backs, J. Leytham, H. Jenkins (Wigan), P. Thomas (Leeds), and W. Batten (Hunslet). Half-backs, T. White (Oldham) and J. Jolley (Run- corn). Forwards, J. Spencer (Salford), A. Holder (Hull), J. Birch (Leeds), H. Wilson (Hamlet), El. Clampitt (Broughton Rangers), and A Smith (Oldham). Reserves Cl-. Thomas and G. Dickenson (Warring ton).</p>
      </div>
